window.__hvBdRegister("nonnus-dionysiaca", {"key":"nonnus-dionysiaca","sections":[{"i":0,"j":321,"loc":"Book I, lines 1.1–1.320","title":"Proem, Europa's rape, Typhon storms heaven","summary":"The poet asks the Muse to sing the double birth of Dionysus and calls on shape-shifting Proteus, whose forms stand for the many episodes of his hymn. He begins with Cadmus: Zeus as a bull carries Europa across the sea, watched by sea-gods and an astonished Achaean sailor, while Europa laments her abduction. Cadmus, searching for her, reaches the Arimi just when Zeus, gone to Plouto's bed, has hidden his weapons in a cave. Typhon, urged by Earth, steals the thunderbolts, assaults the constellations and the sea, and hurls islands, but the bolts slip from his unfamiliar hands.","ideas":["Proteus' changing shapes as an emblem of the poem's varied subject","Europa carried over the sea on the bull, a 'living ship'","Typhon steals Zeus's weapons and wars on the stars and sea","The thunderbolts refuse a stranger's grip, like a horse rejecting a new rider"],"terms":[{"t":"thyrsus","d":"The ivy-wreathed fennel staff of Dionysus, which the poet asks to hold as he sings."},{"t":"Typhon","d":"The many-headed, two-hundred-handed Cilician son of Earth who seizes Zeus's thunderbolts."}]},{"i":322,"j":535,"loc":"Book I, lines 1.321–1.534","title":"Zeus and Cadmus trick Typhon","summary":"On Crete Zeus sets Europa down, Hera mocks his bull-shape, and he takes Europa as a young man; she conceives twins and is given to Asterion, and the Bull is placed among the stars. Zeus, with Eros and Pan's flocks, disguises Cadmus as a herdsman and asks him to charm Typhon with his pipe, promising him Harmonia as wife. Typhon, enchanted, boasts of ruling Olympus and offers the 'shepherd' stars and goddesses for a victory song. Cadmus claims he could do better with a lyre, and Typhon fetches Zeus's lost sinews for strings, which Cadmus hides; then he plays on as Typhon sits spellbound.","ideas":["Europa's union with Zeus and the origin of the constellation Bull","Zeus's plan: Cadmus will save cosmic harmony and win Harmonia","Typhon's boastful offer of heavenly rewards to the herdsman","Cadmus wins back Zeus's sinews by guile"],"terms":[{"t":"Harmonia","d":"Daughter of Ares and Aphrodite promised to Cadmus, with a play on the 'harmony' of the cosmos he will save."},{"t":"Adrasteia","d":"The goddess of inescapable retribution, who records Typhon's boast."}]},{"i":536,"j":773,"loc":"Book II, lines 2.1–2.236","title":"Typhon's rampage and the night before battle","summary":"While Cadmus pipes, Zeus slips back into the cave and recovers his thunderbolts; Typhon finds the cave empty and attacks Olympus. He devours beasts, dries rivers, uproots trees and lays waste farms, and the gods mourn their ruined plants. Two Hamadryads fleeing their trees lament, one fearing every refuge from lustful gods and imagining one transformation after another. Night falls as the stars stand guard over Olympus with omens of fire, and Victory, in Leto's form, urges Zeus to fight, listing the gods who have fled and the threat to Athena and Artemis.","ideas":["Zeus recovers his weapons while Typhon is spellbound","Typhon's devastation of land, river and orchard","The Hamadryads' lament over the dangers of maidenhood","Victory's speech rousing Zeus as the cosmos falls into disorder"],"terms":[{"t":"Hamadryad","d":"A tree nymph whose life is bound to her tree; here they flee as Typhon fells the woods."},{"t":"Nike (Victory)","d":"The goddess who comes to Zeus disguised as Leto and later drives his chariot."}]},{"i":774,"j":1099,"loc":"Book II, lines 2.237–2.562","title":"Typhon's boast and the battle with Zeus","summary":"At dawn Typhon roars in all his beast voices and declares what he will do: overturn heaven, enslave the winds and gods, marry Hera, free Cronus and the Titans, and build an eighth heaven. Zeus laughs and the battle joins, with Strife beside Typhon and Victory beside Zeus. Typhon builds an armour of mountains and hurls rocks, trees and river water, while Zeus fights with thunder, hail, lightning and the four winds. A passage explains how lightning and thunderbolts are born from clouds; Earth prays in vain for her son, and Typhon falls, burned, to the ground.","ideas":["Typhon's long threat to remake the cosmos and the divine order","Mountains as Typhon's weapons against the elements of Zeus","A natural account of how lightning forms in the clouds","Earth's grief as Typhon is brought down"],"terms":[{"t":"Enyo","d":"Goddess of war, whose 'melody' Zeus sounds with the crash of thunder."}]},{"i":1100,"j":1249,"loc":"Book II, lines 2.563–2.712","title":"Zeus mocks Typhon and rewards Cadmus","summary":"Zeus taunts the fallen Typhon, throwing back his boasts, and assigns him burial under Sicily with a mocking epitaph. Taurus and Cydnus celebrate, while Earth mourns by shearing her trees; Typhon's remains are said to cause storms at sea and on land. Nature repairs the earth and the stars return to their places. Zeus then promises Cadmus marriage to Harmonia with the gods at his wedding, warns him against angering Ares, tells him his brothers are safe, and sends him to Delphi; the gods return to Olympus and Typhon's weapons are hung up as a warning.","ideas":["Zeus's mocking speech and Typhon's burial under Sicily","Order restored on earth and among the stars","Zeus's promises and warnings to Cadmus","The fate of Cadmus' brothers Cepheus, Thasos, Cilix and Phineus"],"terms":[{"t":"Pytho","d":"Delphi, where Zeus sends Cadmus to receive Apollo's oracle."}]},{"i":1250,"j":1484,"loc":"Book III, lines 3.1–3.233","title":"Cadmus reaches Samothrace and Electra's palace","summary":"In spring Cadmus sails from Cilicia and comes after ten days to Samothrace, where Harmonia is being kept for him. Woken by the Corybants' dance, he goes into the city led by Persuasion disguised as a woman with a pitcher, and a crow mocks him for being a slow bridegroom. He admires Electra's palace, made by Hephaestus, with its orchard and golden statues of youths and dogs. Her son Emathion, ruling while his brother Dardanus has settled near Ida (after the third flood, following those of Ogygus and Deucalion), welcomes Cadmus to a feast.","ideas":["Spring voyage to Samothrace and the Corybantic rites","Persuasion guides Cadmus; the crow foretells his marriage","Hephaestus' palace of Electra and its garden","Dardanus and the three great floods"],"terms":[{"t":"Cabeiri","d":"Gods of the Samothracian mysteries, whose ritual cry the dancing beasts imitate."},{"t":"Corybants","d":"Armed dancers whose clashing shields and flutes wake Cadmus at dawn."}]},{"i":1485,"j":1695,"loc":"Book III, lines 3.234–3.444","title":"Cadmus' lineage and Hermes' message","summary":"After the feast Cadmus tells Electra his ancestry: from Inachus and Io, who wandered to Egypt, through Epaphus, Libya and Belus, to Agenor, Aegyptus and Danaus, and finally the loss of Europa that set him wandering. Electra consoles him by recalling her own griefs: separation from her Pleiad sisters, Dardanus' departure, and Atlas' burden, and she urges him to settle abroad as others of Io's line have done. The poet tells how Harmonia, born secretly to Ares and Aphrodite, was nursed by Electra beside Emathion. Hermes, disguised as a youth and seen only by Electra, tells her that Zeus wants Harmonia given to Cadmus without bride-price.","ideas":["Cadmus' genealogy from Io to Agenor","Mortal generations compared to leaves","Electra's consolation and the promise of her stellar future","Hermes' command that Harmonia wed Cadmus"],"terms":[{"t":"Pleiades","d":"The seven daughters of Atlas, among them Electra, who will one day become stars."}]},{"i":1696,"j":1945,"loc":"Book IV, lines 4.1–4.248","title":"Harmonia persuaded and the departure","summary":"Electra tells Harmonia of Zeus's command, but she refuses to marry a penniless wandering stranger and doubts Hermes' story. Aphrodite, wearing her cestus and disguised as a neighbour girl Peisinoe, describes Cadmus' beauty, says he must be a god, and begs to have him herself. Goaded by desire, Harmonia says farewell to her home and family with many kisses and tears and goes with Cadmus. Selene mocks Aphrodite for wounding her own daughter, and they sail, a fellow passenger taking the couple for Eros and Aphrodite.","ideas":["Harmonia's refusal of a wedding without gifts","Aphrodite's deceptive speech as Peisinoe","Harmonia's divided mind and farewell to Samothrace","Selene's taunt and the voyage to Greece"],"terms":[{"t":"kestos (cestus)","d":"Aphrodite's embroidered girdle that kindles desire, used to bend Harmonia's will."}]},{"i":1946,"j":2160,"loc":"Book IV, lines 4.249–4.463","title":"Letters, Delphi, the serpent, the Sown Men","summary":"In Greece Cadmus gives the Greeks writing, vowels joined with consonants, a gift the poet ranks above Danaus' wells, together with Egyptian lore, rites of Osiris and astronomy. At Delphi the oracle tells him to stop seeking the bull and to follow a cow and found a city where she lies down. He follows her through Phocis and Boeotia past places of old myths until she sinks down. Seeking water for the sacrifice at Dirce, he meets Ares' serpent, which kills many companions; Athena encourages him, he kills it with a stone, and on her instruction sows its teeth, and the armed earth-born men kill one another when he throws a stone among them.","ideas":["Cadmus brings alphabetic writing to Greece","The Delphic oracle of the cow and the new Thebes","The fight with Ares' serpent at Dirce","The Sown Men rise and destroy one another"],"terms":[{"t":"Spartoi (Sown Men)","d":"Armed men sprung from the serpent's teeth; five are to be left alive for Thebes."}]},{"i":2161,"j":2351,"loc":"Book V, lines 5.1–5.189","title":"Founding of Thebes and Harmonia's wedding","summary":"Cadmus sacrifices the cow to Athena, feasts his men, and defeats the Ectenes, Aonians and Temmices. He lays out Thebes in streets aligned with the four winds, builds temples and houses, and gives it seven gates named for the seven planets, leaving the wall for Amphion. The gods attend the wedding of Cadmus and Harmonia: Ares dances unarmed, Apollo and the Muses sing, and Zeus shares Cadmus' table. The gods give gifts, above all Aphrodite's jewelled necklace made by Hephaestus in the shape of a two-headed serpent around an eagle.","ideas":["Thebes laid out as an earthly image of the seven-zoned heaven","Gates assigned to the planets","The gods dance and feast at the wedding","Hephaestus' serpent necklace, given by Aphrodite"],"terms":[{"t":"Oncaean gate","d":"The Theban gate given to the moon and named, the poet says, from the lowing of the cow."}]},{"i":2352,"j":2571,"loc":"Book V, lines 5.190–5.409","title":"Cadmus' children, Aristaeus, Actaeon's death","summary":"Harmonia bears Autonoe, Ino, Agave and Semele, and later Polydorus. Autonoe marries Aristaeus, son of Apollo and Cyrene, whose inventions are listed: hunting with hounds and nets, beekeeping, olive pressing, herding, and the sacrifice that brought the Etesian winds against Sirius. Their son Actaeon, a great hunter, watches Artemis bathing from a tree; she turns him into a stag, keeping his human mind, and his own hounds tear him slowly apart while he laments. Autonoe hears of his death and searches the hills, but fails to recognize the deer's bones.","ideas":["The daughters of Cadmus and their marriages","Aristaeus as inventor of rustic arts","Actaeon sees Artemis bathing and is turned into a stag","Autonoe's vain search for her son's body"],"terms":[{"t":"Aristaeus","d":"Son of Apollo and Cyrene, also called Nomios and Agreus, who taught hunting, beekeeping and herding."}]},{"i":2572,"j":2783,"loc":"Book V, lines 5.410–5.621","title":"Actaeon's ghost; Zeus desires Persephone","summary":"Actaeon's ghost, in deer form, appears to Aristaeus in a dream, explains his metamorphosis, begs pity for the hounds, and asks for burial with his bow and arrows. Autonoe finds the tree, the weapons and the bones, and buries him. Agave bears Pentheus to Echion, and Ino bears Learchus and Melicertes and later nurses Dionysus. The poet turns to the earlier Dionysus, Zagreus: all the gods court Persephone, and Zeus, seeing her admire herself in a mirror and bathe, falls in love with her and abandons all his other loves.","ideas":["Actaeon's dream speech and burial","Births of Pentheus, Learchus and Melicertes","Zeus's plan to beget a new Dionysus after Zagreus","Zeus's passion for Persephone"],"terms":[{"t":"Zagreus","d":"The 'ancient-born' horned Dionysus, son of Zeus and Persephone, of whom the later Dionysus will be an image."}]},{"i":2784,"j":2939,"loc":"Book VI, lines 6.1–6.154","title":"Demeter consults Astraeus and hides Persephone","summary":"Demeter, alarmed by her daughter's many suitors and above all by Hephaestus, visits the astrologer Astraeus. His sons the Winds serve her a feast, and then he casts Persephone's horoscope with a celestial sphere. He warns of a secret ravisher in beast form and promises Demeter fame for giving grain to the earth. Demeter drives her serpent chariot to Sicily and hides Persephone in a stone cave, guarded by the serpents, where the girl spins and weaves.","ideas":["Astraeus reads the stars on a model sphere","The oracle of a hidden bridegroom and of Demeter's harvests","Persephone hidden in a Sicilian cave guarded by serpents"],"terms":[{"t":"Astraeus","d":"Old astrologer god, father of the Winds, who casts Persephone's horoscope."}]},{"i":2940,"j":3173,"loc":"Book VI, lines 6.155–6.388","title":"Zagreus killed; fire and flood","summary":"Zeus, as a serpent, enters the cave and fathers Zagreus on Persephone; the horned infant sits on Zeus's throne and handles his lightning. Sent by Hera, the Titans, faces whitened with gypsum, attack him as he gazes in a mirror; he changes into many shapes but, as a bull, is cut to pieces. Zeus imprisons the Titans in Tartarus and sets the world ablaze, then at Ocean's plea floods the earth, with sea creatures on mountains, Pan and Galatea talking, and Alpheius lamenting. Deucalion survives in his chest, Poseidon splits the Thessalian mountains so the water drains, and cities are rebuilt.","ideas":["Birth of Zagreus and his brief rule with the thunderbolt","The Titans' murder of Zagreus through his transformations","World conflagration followed by the universal flood","Deucalion's survival and the renewal of the earth"],"terms":[{"t":"Titans","d":"The older gods who, at Hera's urging, dismember the infant Zagreus."},{"t":"Deucalion","d":"The survivor of the great flood, sailing in a chest."}]},{"i":3174,"j":3310,"loc":"Book VII, lines 7.1–7.135","title":"Aeon's plea and Zeus's promise of wine","summary":"Human life, without wine, is full of toil and joyless. Aeon, the aged shepherd of time, kneels before Zeus and complains of war, old age and death, saying the gods should have given mortals nectar and threatening to give up the helm of time. Zeus answers that he will give men wine through a son born from his own thigh, a rival to Demeter, who after his labours and the Indian war will join the stars. Eros takes from his quiver the fifth of Zeus's twelve love-arrows, the one for Semele, and wreathes it with ivy.","ideas":["Aeon's lament for suffering humanity","Wine as a remedy for human cares","Zeus foretells Dionysus' gifts and deification","Eros' quiver with twelve arrows for Zeus's loves"],"terms":[{"t":"Aiōn (Aeon)","d":"The personified age or eternity, holder of the key of generation, who petitions Zeus."}]},{"i":3311,"j":3543,"loc":"Book VII, lines 7.136–7.368","title":"Semele's dream, her bath, and Zeus","summary":"Semele dreams of a fruit-laden tree struck by heavenly fire, its fruit carried by a bird to Zeus, who sews it into his thigh. Teiresias advises a sacrifice to Zeus, and, spattered with blood, she goes to the Asopus to wash. Eros shoots Zeus, who sees her bathing, recalls Europa, and watches her as an eagle while a Naiad wonders whether she is Aphrodite, Selene or Athena. Zeus impatiently urges the sun to set, comes to Semele at night in the shapes of bull, lion, leopard and serpent, and promises her a son, Dionysus, who will bring joy to gods and men.","ideas":["Semele's prophetic dream of the burned tree","Zeus's desire kindled as she bathes in the Asopus","Zeus's love-sick speech to Helios and Selene","The union in animal shapes that foreshadow Dionysus"],"terms":[{"t":"Teiresias","d":"The Theban seer, son of Chariclo, who interprets Semele's dream."}]},{"i":3544,"j":3724,"loc":"Book VIII, lines 1–179","title":"Envy rouses Hera, who borrows Deceit's girdle","summary":"Semele, pregnant by Zeus, raves in Bacchic fashion at every pipe, cymbal and bellowing bull, and the unborn child leaps with her. Envy, jealous of the coming Bacchus, takes the shape of Ares and taunts Athena and Hera: Zeus has left heaven for an earthly bride, and Ares threatens to quit Olympus for Thrace rather than watch Zeus's bastards enthroned there. Hera, enraged, searches the cities for Deceit and finds her in Crete by the false tomb of Zeus. Recalling the wrongs done her by Zeus's earlier loves and claiming she must win back her son Ares, she asks for Deceit's girdle of charms, and Deceit gives it to her.","ideas":["Semele's pregnancy already Bacchic: the unborn god dances in the womb","Envy in the guise of Ares stirs Hera and Athena to jealousy","Hera's grievance list of Zeus's affairs (Danae, Europa, Io, Leto)","Deceit's Cydonian girdle holds every trick and false oath"],"terms":[{"t":"Apatē (Deceit)","d":"Personified Guile, who lives among the Cretans because they are liars and lends Hera her all-charming girdle."},{"t":"Phthonos (Envy)","d":"Personified jealousy, poisoned by his own venom, who poses as Ares to stir up Hera."}]},{"i":3725,"j":3963,"loc":"Book VIII, lines 180–418","title":"Semele asks for the thunderbolt and dies","summary":"Hera, disguised as the old nurse of Cadmus, visits pale, pregnant Semele and asks which god has seduced her, hinting that a true lover would come with his tokens and that Zeus should come with his thunderbolt as he does to Hera. Back in heaven Hera urges the idle weapons of Zeus to become Semele's murderers. Semele, ashamed before Cadmus and the citizens, begs Zeus to come to her in his fiery form as he comes to Hera. Zeus warns her the bolts are not gentle, but cannot contend with the Fates; he comes in lightning, Semele boasts and touches the bolt, and is burned to ash. The fire spares the unripe child, Hermes carries him to his father, and Semele is raised to Olympus.","ideas":["Hera's disguise as the nurse plants doubt and the wish for the thunderbolt","Semele demands the pattern of Hera's 'fire-crowned bed'","Bridal chamber becomes pyre and tomb; lightning acts as midwife","Semele made immortal, sharing the table of the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"Eileithyia","d":"Goddess of childbirth; here the thunderbolts themselves are said to play her part in delivering Bacchus."},{"t":"Thyone","d":"Another name used for Semele as mother of Dionysus."}]},{"i":3964,"j":4096,"loc":"Book IX, lines 1–131","title":"Thigh-birth of Dionysus and his first nurses","summary":"Zeus sews the half-formed child into his thigh and brings him to birth at the due time; the Seasons crown him with ivy and serpents. Hermes names him Dionysus (from the limping of Zeus, 'nysos' meaning lame) and Eiraphiotes (the sewn-in one), and gives him to the daughters of Lamus, river nymphs who suckle him. Hera drives them mad, and as they are about to cut the child to pieces Hermes snatches him away to Ino, who is nursing Melicertes. Hermes foretells Ino's future as the sea goddess Leucothea, and Ino nurses both infants. Her handmaid Mystis keeps the child in a dark chamber lit by his own radiance and invents the rites of Dionysus: rattle, cymbals, torch, thyrsus, fawnskin and mystic chest.","ideas":["Birth from the male thigh; the names Dionysus and Eiraphiotes explained","Hera maddens the daughters of Lamus","Hermes' prophecy of Ino as Leucothea and Melicertes as Palaemon","Mystis invents the instruments and rites of the mysteries"],"terms":[{"t":"Eiraphiotes","d":"Cult title of Dionysus, explained here as 'sewn in', from his time in Zeus's thigh."},{"t":"Mystis","d":"Ino's Sidonian handmaid, after whom the art of the mysteries is named."},{"t":"thyrsus","d":"The ivy-wrapped Bacchic staff, devised by Mystis with an iron point hidden in its leaves."}]},{"i":4097,"j":4286,"loc":"Book IX, lines 132–321","title":"Rhea rears Dionysus; Ino's madness and wandering","summary":"Hera finds the child in Ino's house and vows to ruin it; Hermes carries him off, frightens Hera away by taking the shape of Phanes, and gives the boy to Rhea. Among the Corybants Dionysus grows up hunting, carrying beasts, driving Rhea's lion chariot and taming a bear, to the delight of Rhea and Zeus. In heaven Semele boasts over Hera that Zeus himself bore her son and Rhea nursed him. Hera then maddens Ino, who runs wild over the hills to Delphi, terrifying the Pythian priestess and shepherds, until Apollo calms her with sleep and ambrosia and she stays there four years. Meanwhile Athamas forgets her, marries Themisto, and has sons by her.","ideas":["Hermes takes the form of Phanes to stop Hera","Boyhood of Dionysus in Phrygia with Rhea and the Corybants","Semele's boast: the father both sowed and bore the child","Ino's mad wandering to Delphi; Athamas' third marriage to Themisto"],"terms":[{"t":"Phanes","d":"The first-born god of Orphic tradition, whose shape Hermes borrows to cow Hera."},{"t":"Corybants","d":"Rhea's armed dancers who guard the young god by clashing swords on shields."}]},{"i":4287,"j":4426,"loc":"Book X, lines 1–138","title":"Madness of Athamas and Ino's leap into the sea","summary":"Themisto has killed her own sons by mistake, and Athamas, driven mad by Pan's whip and the Erinyes, attacks his flocks, takes a goat for Ino, shoots at empty air, and sees phantoms of Megaera and Artemis. Ino returns after four years to find him insane. Taking his son Learchus for a stag, he shoots him and crushes his head, then puts the infant Melicertes in a boiling cauldron. Ino snatches the half-burnt child and flees. At the shore she reproaches Zeus for the reward she got for nursing Bacchus, blames Nephele's Erinyes, and leaps into the sea with her son. Poseidon receives her as Leucothea, a sea goddess, and in heaven Semele mocks her sister's watery lot.","ideas":["Athamas' hallucinations under Pan and the Erinyes","Learchus killed as a stag; Melicertes put in the cauldron","Ino's lament and leap, becoming Leucothea","Semele's taunt: heaven for her son, the sea for Ino's"],"terms":[{"t":"Leucothea","d":"The 'White Goddess', Ino's name as a sea divinity, here tied to her running across the White plain."},{"t":"Erinyes","d":"Avenging goddesses (Megaera, Tisiphone) whose phantoms madden Athamas."}]},{"i":4427,"j":4718,"loc":"Book X, lines 139–430","title":"Dionysus and Ampelus: love, wrestling and the race","summary":"Grown in Lydia, Dionysus bathes in the golden Pactolus with his Satyrs and falls in love with the beautiful Satyr youth Ampelus. He asks the boy's parentage, guessing at divine fathers, and becomes wholly absorbed in him, jealous of rivals and afraid that Zephyrus, Zeus or Poseidon may snatch him as they did Hyacinthus, Ganymede and Pelops. He prays Zeus to grant him only Ampelus, not the lightning. The two wrestle on the river sand for a flute as prize, and Dionysus lets himself be thrown, as Zeus once yielded to Heracles. He then sets a foot race between Ampelus, Cissus and Leneus; Cissus slips, Leneus is checked, and with the god's help Ampelus wins.","ideas":["Ampelus' beauty compared to the Moon and spring","Dionysus' jealous fears drawn from earlier divine loves","The wrestling match, which Dionysus loses willingly","The race won by Ampelus with divine help; Leneus suspects favour"],"terms":[{"t":"Ampelus","d":"Satyr youth loved by Dionysus; his name means 'vine'."},{"t":"Pactolus","d":"Lydian river with gold-bearing sands where Dionysus and the Satyrs bathe."}]},{"i":4719,"j":4945,"loc":"Book XI, lines 1–225","title":"The swimming match and Ampelus killed by a bull","summary":"Dionysus invites Ampelus to a third contest, a swim in the Pactolus, and lets him win. Ampelus starts to copy the god, dressing like him and riding bears, lions and tigers; Dionysus warns him to fear only the bull's horns. An omen follows: a horned serpent kills a fawn on an altar, and Dionysus both grieves for the boy and laughs for the sake of the wine to come. Ate, disguised as a youth, taunts Ampelus that Dionysus has given him no chariot and urges him to ride a bull as Europa did. He decorates a wild bull and mounts it, boasting to Selene that he is now a horned driver of cattle. Selene sends a gadfly; the bull throws him on the rocks and gores him to death.","ideas":["Dionysus' warning: fear only the bull","The omen of the serpent and the fawn","Ate tempts Ampelus to ride the bull","Ampelus' boast angers Selene, who sends the gadfly"],"terms":[{"t":"Ate","d":"Goddess of ruinous folly, here acting for Hera, the stepmother of Dionysus."},{"t":"Mene / Selene","d":"The Moon goddess, a horned driver of cattle, whom Ampelus offends by his boast."}]},{"i":4946,"j":5241,"loc":"Book XI, lines 226–521","title":"Lament for Ampelus; tale of Calamus and Carpus","summary":"Dionysus runs to the body, dresses it in his fawnskin and robe, cuts a lock of his hair and pours Rhea's ambrosia on the wounds; the dead boy keeps his beauty. In a long lament he envies Apollo his Hyacinthus, vows to kill the bull and every beast that might have killed the boy, and asks Zeus to let Ampelus speak once more. Eros, in the shape of Silenus, consoles him with the tale of Calamus, son of the Maeander, and his friend Carpus: Carpus drowned in a swimming race, and Calamus followed him into the river; one became the reed and the other the fruit of the field. Dionysus is still grieving as the four Seasons, described in turn, set out for the house of Helios.","ideas":["Funeral honours and ambrosia on the corpse","Dionysus' wish to be mortal and follow the boy into Hades","Eros' advice: a new love cures an old one","Calamus and Carpus become reed and fruit; the four Seasons go to Helios"],"terms":[{"t":"Calamus","d":"Son of the river Maeander who becomes the reed (calamus) after drowning himself."},{"t":"Carpus","d":"Youth whose name means 'fruit', drowned by the wind and turned into the fruit of the field."},{"t":"Horai (Seasons)","d":"Daughters of the year, each pictured with her own weather and fruits; the autumn Season still lacks the vine."}]},{"i":5242,"j":5414,"loc":"Book XII, lines 1–171","title":"The tablets of Harmonia and Atropos' promise","summary":"The Seasons reach the house of Helios, and the autumn Season asks when the vine will grow and to which god it is pledged. Helios points her to the prophetic tablets of Harmonia, inscribed by Phanes. She reads of the reign of Ophion and Cronus and Zeus's victory, of the flood of Deucalion, and of coming transformations (Argus, Philomela, Niobe, Atalanta and others), then the oracle that Cissus will become ivy, Calamus the reed and Ampelus the vine, and that Zeus assigns the vine to Dionysus. Meanwhile Dionysus mourns, and rivers, Niobe and trees grieve with him. Atropos then declares that Ampelus will not cross Acheron but will become wine, a drink to delight the whole world and ease mortal tears.","ideas":["The Season's question: who will have the vine?","Harmonia's tablets record the history and fated changes of the world","Oracle: Cissus, Calamus and Ampelus turned into plants","Atropos: Dionysus wept so that he may end mortal tears"],"terms":[{"t":"Atropos","d":"One of the Fates, who here reverses her threads to promise Ampelus' change into wine."},{"t":"tablets of Harmonia","d":"Cosmic tablets, written by Phanes, holding all the oracles fated for the world."}]},{"i":5415,"j":5640,"loc":"Book XII, lines 172–397","title":"Ampelus becomes the vine; the first vintage","summary":"The corpse of Ampelus turns into a vine, each part becoming a branch, root or cluster, and Cissus becomes the ivy that winds around it. Dionysus squeezes the first grapes in his hand and drinks from an ox horn, then praises the boy's fate and the vine above every other plant, taunting Apollo, Athena and Demeter that their gifts cannot banish grief as wine does. The poet adds an older tale: the vine first grew wild from Olympian ichor, and Dionysus, seeing a serpent drink grape juice, understood Rhea's oracles, dug a wine press in the rock, and with the Satyrs held the first vintage. The drunken Satyrs chase nymphs and Bacchants, Rhea gives Dionysus the amethyst against drunkenness, and he teaches his festival to Maeonia.","ideas":["Metamorphosis of Ampelus into the vine and Cissus into ivy","First wine pressed by hand and drunk from a horn","Dionysus' boast of wine over laurel, olive and corn","The older tale of the serpent and the first wine press"],"terms":[{"t":"keras (horn)","d":"The ox horn used as the first cup, from which the text derives a name for mixed wine."},{"t":"amethyst","d":"Stone that Rhea gives Dionysus alone as a guard against drunken madness."}]},{"i":5641,"j":5863,"loc":"Book XIII, lines 1–221","title":"Call to the Indian war; catalogue of Greek contingents","summary":"Zeus sends Iris to Rhea's cave to tell Dionysus to drive the unjust, impious Indians and their king Deriades out of Asia and teach the nations his rites and wine: he must earn Olympus by toil as Hermes, Apollo and Zeus did. Rhea sends Pyrrhichus to gather an army, and the poet, calling on the Muses and Homer, begins a catalogue. Actaeon leads Boeotians from many towns; Hymenaeus, beloved of Dionysus, leads another Boeotian force; Phocians come from Delphi and Parnassus; the seven Corybants who tended the infant god lead the Euboeans; Erechtheus leads the Athenians; and Aeacus leads the Myrmidons, once ants, with a shield showing Zeus as an eagle carrying off Aegina.","ideas":["Dionysus must win heaven by labour against the Indians","Catalogue modelled on Homer's Catalogue of Ships","Local myths attached to each town (Orion, Iphigeneia, Triptolemus)","Aeacus' Myrmidons and his shield of the eagle and Aegina"],"terms":[{"t":"Deriades","d":"King of the Indians, son of a river, against whom Dionysus is sent."},{"t":"Pyrrhichus","d":"Rhea's messenger, a dancer of the war-dance, who gathers the host."}]},{"i":5864,"j":6210,"loc":"Book XIII, lines 222–568","title":"Catalogue continued: Crete to Caria","summary":"The catalogue goes on with Asterius, son of Minos, leading the Cretans, with a note that he later settled among the Colchians. Aristaeus comes late, still sore at losing to Dionysus a contest before the gods in which honey was judged against wine; he leads the Arcadians, armed by Apollo. Achates leads Sicilians, followed by Faunus, son of Circe. Crataegonus leads Libyans from the cities Cadmus founded there, with stories of Cadmus defending Harmonia and of Psyllus' fatal war against the south wind. Ogyrus and Oeagrus bring Thracians from Samos and Pimpleia, Litrus and Lapethus Cypriots, Stabius and Stamnus Lydians (with a tale of a priest binding Typhon by a word), Priasus Phrygians, and Miletus and Caunus Carians.","ideas":["The contest of honey and wine, won by Dionysus","Cadmus and Harmonia in Libya; the hundred Libyan cities","Typhon held fast by the spell of a Lydian priest","Priasus' exile in Boeotia during the Phrygian flood"],"terms":[{"t":"Aristaeus","d":"Son of Apollo and Cyrene, the beekeeper who lost the contest of gifts to Dionysus."},{"t":"Ogyrus","d":"Giant-like Thracian leader who swears to destroy the Indian land alone."}]},{"i":6211,"j":6439,"loc":"Book XIV, lines 1–227","title":"Rhea gathers the divine host","summary":"Rhea flies through the world summoning nymphs and spirits for the war, and the poet catalogues the divine army. The Cabeiri Alcon and Eurymedon, sons of Hephaestus, come from Lemnos and Samothrace; the Idaean Dactyls and Corybants from Crete; the Telchines from the sea; the Centaurs Pholus and Chiron; and the Cyclopes, though Polyphemus stays behind for love of Galatea. Twelve Pans come, along with Agreus and Nomius, sons of Hermes; then old Silenus and his sons Astraeus, Maron and Leneus, and the Satyr leaders, who are bold with wine but cowards in battle. Two races of horned Centaurs follow: the daughters of Lamus, whom Hera transformed for nursing Dionysus, and the Cyprian Centaurs born from Zeus's seed spilt on the earth. Last come nymphs, Meliae and the named Bassarids.","ideas":["Rhea's summons across the four quarters of the world","Polyphemus kept from the war by love of Galatea","The Satyrs described as lions at drink and hares in war","Hera changes Dionysus' nurses into horned Centaurs"],"terms":[{"t":"Cabeiri","d":"Smith gods, sons of Hephaestus and Cabeiro, worshipped in mysteries on Lemnos and Samothrace."},{"t":"Bassarids","d":"Bacchants or maenads, the women followers of Dionysus; several are named as his nurses."}]},{"i":6440,"j":6649,"loc":"Book XIV, lines 228–437","title":"The march and first battle at the Astacid lake","summary":"Dionysus leads the host unarmed in the ordinary sense: serpents in his hair, buskins, a starry fawnskin, a horn of wine and a thyrsus, with wagons of vine shoots and wine jars behind. Passing Niobe's rock, he hears the stone warn the Indians not to fight a son of Zeus. Ascania accepts his rites peacefully. He sends heralds to the Indians, but Hera in Indian shape stirs Astraeis and Celaeneus to reject them, and the heralds are driven off. The armies clash by the Astacid lake, the Indians screaming like cranes; the Bassarids fight with thyrsi, serpents and cymbals and tear beasts apart. Many Indians fall, and Dionysus, pitying the rest, turns the lake to wine; an Indian drinks and marvels at the drink.","ideas":["Dionysus' battle gear of ivy, fawnskin and wine horn","Niobe's stone warns the Indians","Hera makes the Indians refuse the heralds","The lake turned to wine at the end of the battle"],"terms":[{"t":"Astacid lake","d":"Lake near Astacia in Bithynia, site of the first battle with the Indians."},{"t":"Astraeis","d":"Indian leader who, urged by Hera, mistreats the heralds of Dionysus."}]},{"i":6650,"j":6819,"loc":"Book XV, lines 1–168","title":"The drunken Indians are captured asleep","summary":"The Indians crowd to the wine-lake and drink in every posture until they see double. In their drunkenness they attack bulls, goats, deer and trees as if these were Dionysus, Pan, Satyrs and Bassarids, and others beat drums, play flutes and dance like Corybants. An Indian who tries to rape a Bacchant flees when a serpent from her bosom coils around his neck. Then Sleep falls on all of them, and they lie in every posture under trees and by the river. Dionysus orders his followers to take them alive without bloodshed and make them servants of Rhea. They are bound with serpents, vine shoots and withies and carried off, and Hymenaeus shines in the golden shield of a sleeping enemy.","ideas":["Drunken delusions: beasts and trees mistaken for the god's army","The Bacchant's guardian serpent","Sleep overcomes the whole Indian force","Bloodless capture; captives to serve Rhea's rites"],"terms":[{"t":"Hypnos (Sleep)","d":"God of sleep, said to favour Dionysus as father of Pasithea, and who brings down the drunken Indians."}]},{"i":6820,"j":7073,"loc":"Book XV, lines 169–422","title":"Nicaea the huntress kills the herdsman Hymnus","summary":"Among the Astacid nymphs lives Nicaea, a huntress of lions and bears who scorns love and even blames Artemis for hunting mere deer. The herdsman Hymnus falls in love with her, forgets his cattle and follows her, longing to be her arrow or bowstring and pleading his case with examples of divine loves for herdsmen. She mocks him with Pan, Daphnis and Daphne. Not seeing how loveless she is, he begs her to kill him and asks for a grave marked with her arrow. Enraged, she shoots him through the throat in mid-speech. Nymphs, Naiads, Niobe's rock, Rhea, Echo, beasts and cattle all lament him with the refrain that the fair cowherd has perished, and Eros swears to yoke the unwilling maiden to Dionysus.","ideas":["Nicaea as a second Artemis, hostile to marriage","Hymnus' wooing speeches and his plea to die by her hand","Her arrow cuts off his speech","Nature's lament with refrain; Eros vows vengeance through Dionysus"],"terms":[{"t":"Nicaea","d":"Astacid huntress nymph, a fierce virgin, later mother by Dionysus and namesake of a city."},{"t":"Adrasteia","d":"Goddess of inescapable retribution, who sees the killing and shows it to Aphrodite."}]},{"i":7074,"j":7257,"loc":"Book XVI, lines 1–182","title":"Eros wounds Dionysus; Nicaea rejects him","summary":"To avenge Hymnus, Eros shoots Dionysus as Nicaea bathes, and the god falls in love with her. He imagines courting her as a suppliant with a vine cluster, wishes he could take the forms Zeus used for Europa, Aegina and Danae, sees her beauty in the meadow flowers, and makes a long speech offering to carry her nets, make her bed with skins, give her hounds, handmaids and the Graces, and have her hunt or fight at his side. When he calls on her to stay and take him as husband, she answers angrily that he must first win Athena or Artemis, threatens to wound or chain him as the Aloadae chained Ares, scorns him as a soft, womanish god, and runs off.","ideas":["Eros' revenge for Hymnus","Dionysus wishes for Zeus's transformations","His catalogue of gifts and promises to Nicaea","Nicaea's scorn for a 'womanish' bridegroom"],"terms":[{"t":"Amazon","d":"Used of Nicaea as a warlike, man-scorning huntress."}]},{"i":7258,"j":7480,"loc":"Book XVI, lines 183–405","title":"Nicaea made drunk and wedded; birth of Telete","summary":"Dionysus searches for Nicaea with a hound from Pan, promising it a place among the stars, and a Meliad nymph mocks him for pleading when Zeus took his brides without asking. Thirsty from the heat, Nicaea drinks from the wine-river, becomes drunk and falls asleep; Eros shows her to Dionysus, who loosens her girdle and lies with her as the earth grows a vine canopy over them and Echo and Pan sing a wedding song. The ghost of Hymnus taunts her in a dream. Pan and a Satyr envy the trick. Nicaea wakes, laments her lost maidenhood, thinks of suicide, and shoots in vain at the god's tracks. She bears a daughter, Telete, and Dionysus founds the city of Nicaea by the lake.","ideas":["The Meliad's mockery of a suppliant Dionysus","Wine and sleep used to overcome the unwilling maiden","Hymnus' ghost: the one who killed a lover is wedded without consent","Birth of Telete and founding of Nicaea"],"terms":[{"t":"Telete","d":"Daughter of Dionysus and Nicaea, a night-dancing girl whose name means 'initiation' or 'rite'."},{"t":"Meliad","d":"Ash-tree nymph who lives inside her tree."}]},{"i":7481,"j":7649,"loc":"Book XVII, lines 1–167","title":"Brongus' hospitality and the Pans' battle","summary":"Leaving the sleeping Indians captive, Dionysus marches on toward Deriades and reaches Alybe by the river Geudis. The countryman Brongus receives him in a rock dwelling with milk, olives and cheese; the god stops him from sacrificing a sheep, then repays him with wine and teaches him to tend the vine. Astraeis, the one Indian who did not drink, reports the drugged river and the bloodless capture to Orontes, son-in-law of Deriades, and warns him against the drink. As Orontes returns to the fight, the Bassarids, Bacchoi and Pans fall on the Indians above Taurus with hurled hills, horns, hooves and Deo's sickle.","ideas":["Brongus' plain table recalls Molorchus feeding Heracles","Wine given as a gift that frees from care, better than milk","Astraeis reports the drug-victory of the river","Pans fight with horns, hooves and a reaping sickle"],"terms":[{"t":"Brongus","d":"A shepherd of Alybe who hosts Dionysus in a natural rock house and is taught viticulture in return."},{"t":"Orontes","d":"Son-in-law of Deriades and leader of the Indian army in this book."}]},{"i":7650,"j":7879,"loc":"Book XVII, lines 168–397","title":"Orontes' duel with Bacchus and suicide","summary":"Orontes rallies the Indians with scorn for the womanish god and routs the Satyrs, Sileni and Centaurs, while Erembeus wounds the Bacchant Helice. Dionysus enters the fight; Orontes' blows glance off the god's horn and fawnskin, and a light touch of the vine-cluster splits his breastplate. Calling on Helios to tell Deriades of the defeat, Orontes stabs himself and falls into the river that takes his name; Bacchus mocks the corpse and the Hamadryads bury him under a laurel with an epitaph. The Bacchants then cut down the Indians with ivy and leaves, Aristaeus heals the wounded with herbs, honey and song, and the Erythraean leader Blemys submits and is sent off to rule near Meroe.","ideas":["Orontes cannot wound the god; a vine-cluster breaks his armor","Orontes' suicide names the river Orontes","Leaves and ivy pierce bronze armor","Blemys' supplication founds the Blemyes"],"terms":[{"t":"thyrsus","d":"The ivy-wreathed fennel staff that serves Dionysus and his followers as a weapon."},{"t":"Aristaeus","d":"Son of Apollo who heals the Bassarids' wounds with his father's art."}]},{"i":7880,"j":8046,"loc":"Book XVIII, lines 1–165","title":"Staphylus and Botrys feast Dionysus","summary":"Rumor spreads the fame of Dionysus through Assyria, and king Staphylus with his son Botrys meets him and begs him to be his guest, citing earlier hosts of Zeus such as Lycaon, Tantalus and Macello. Botrys drives ahead to prepare a feast while Staphylus shows the god a palace of gems, marble and gold. Bulls and sheep are slaughtered and the household tastes wine for the first time: Maron dances drunk, queen Methe totters and must be held up, and Staphylus, Botrys and old Pithos all dance. At nightfall the company sleeps.","ideas":["Staphylus invokes famous hosts of the gods","The jewelled Assyrian palace","First taste of wine sets the whole household dancing"],"terms":[{"t":"Staphylus, Botrys, Methe, Pithos","d":"The Assyrian king, his son, his wife and an old servant; their names mean grape-cluster, bunch, drunkenness and wine-jar."}]},{"i":8047,"j":8249,"loc":"Book XVIII, lines 166–368","title":"Warning dream, Staphylus' charge, his death","summary":"At dawn Dionysus wakes from a dream in which a lion drives him into the sea and attacks the Bacchants until a Bacchant binds it, Artemis takes it and lightning blinds it, a foreshadowing of Lycurgus. Staphylus gives him guest-gifts and urges him to fight like his father, telling Belus' tale of Zeus overcoming Cronus and the monster Campe, Ares killing a son of Echidna, and Perseus' recent visit. Dionysus, stirred, sends the herald Pherespondus to give Deriades the choice of wine or war, and spreads the vine through Assyria. Staphylus dies meanwhile, and Dionysus returns to find the household in mourning and questions Methe.","ideas":["Prophetic dream of the lion foreshadows Lycurgus","Campe described as a many-headed Tartarean monster","Ultimatum to Deriades: accept the gifts or fight","Staphylus' sudden death turns the feast to mourning"],"terms":[{"t":"Campe","d":"A many-shaped winged monster in Cronus' war that Zeus kills with the thunderbolt."}]},{"i":8250,"j":8409,"loc":"Book XIX, lines 1–158","title":"Methe consoled; the lyre contest","summary":"Methe tells of Staphylus' death and asks for wine, and after drinking she and Botrys put aside their grief; she offers to follow Dionysus. The god promises her a place as his wine-pourer and names drunkenness after her, the grape-bunch after Botrys and the grape after Staphylus. At Staphylus' tomb he holds funeral games, first a lyre contest with a bull and a goat as prizes. Erechtheus of Athens sings of Demeter consoling Celeus' household, matching the present story; Oeagrus wins with a two-line song saying Dionysus will make Staphylus live forever. Dionysus then sets out two mixing-bowls for a dancing contest.","ideas":["Wine as the cure for grief","Names of wine and the grape taken from the Assyrian family","Erechtheus and Oeagrus compete in song at the tomb","Oeagrus' brief Amyclaean-style song wins"],"terms":[{"t":"Oeagrus","d":"Thracian king and father of Orpheus, victor in the lyre contest."}]},{"i":8410,"j":8599,"loc":"Book XIX, lines 159–348","title":"Maron and Silenus dance; Silenus becomes river","summary":"Maron and Silenus compete in dance for the golden mixing-bowl of old wine. Maron dedicates his dance to Staphylus, refusing to weep, and mimes in silence the wine-pourers of Olympus, Hebe and Ganymede. Silenus mimes a contest of Aristaeus and Dionysus before Zeus, in which honey cloys the gods but wine leaves them wanting more, then performs wild acrobatics until he falls and is changed into a river with reeds for hair. Maron wins, throws the silver bowl into the new river, and addresses it, comparing it with the Silenus who challenged Apollo.","ideas":["Dance as wordless mimicry","Aristaeus' honey versus Dionysus' wine before the gods","Silenus' fall and change into a river","Maron's speech compares the earlier Silenus flayed by Apollo"],"terms":[{"t":"Maron","d":"Aged companion of Dionysus who wins the dancing contest."}]},{"i":8600,"j":8743,"loc":"Book XX, lines 1–142","title":"Strife's rebuke and departure from Assyria","summary":"Feasting continues in Botrys' halls; Dionysus dresses Methe, Pithos and Botrys in fresh robes and all sleep. Strife comes to him in a dream in the form of Rhea and rebukes him for reveling while Deriades waits, contrasting him with the warlike deeds of Athena, Apollo, Hermes and Artemis and telling him the road to Olympus requires toil. Dionysus wakes and the army sets out, some riding bears, bulls and lions, with Botrys, Methe and Pithos following. The poet anticipates Pithos being placed beside a Lydian vat and remembered in the wine jar.","ideas":["Strife shames Dionysus for idleness","Toil is the price of a place in heaven","The army rides wild beasts to war","Pithos commemorated in the wine-jar"],"terms":[{"t":"Eris (Strife)","d":"Personified Strife, who in Rhea's guise urges Dionysus back to war."}]},{"i":8744,"j":9005,"loc":"Book XX, lines 143–404","title":"Lycurgus drives Dionysus into the sea","summary":"Passing through Tyre, Byblos and Lebanon, Dionysus reaches Arabia, ruled by Lycurgus, son of Ares, who sacrifices strangers and hangs their limbs on his gate. Hera sends Iris with an ox-axe; disguised as Ares, Iris urges Lycurgus to fight, then as Hermes persuades Dionysus to come to him unarmed, bearing gifts. Lycurgus mocks the god, scatters the Bacchants with his axe, and Hera's thunder makes Dionysus think Zeus is against him, so he flees into the Erythraean sea, where Thetis and Nereus receive and console him. Lycurgus raves at the sea, and Zeus warns him with the example of Teiresias' blinding.","ideas":["Lycurgus' gate decorated with victims' limbs","Iris deceives both sides by disguise","Dionysus' flight beneath the sea","Zeus warns Lycurgus of blindness"],"terms":[{"t":"Lycurgus","d":"Son of Dryas and of Ares' blood, king in Arabia and enemy of Dionysus."},{"t":"bouplēx (ox-axe)","d":"The axe Hera gives Lycurgus to use against Dionysus."}]},{"i":9006,"j":9176,"loc":"Book XXI, lines 1–169","title":"Ambrosia's vine-bonds and Lycurgus' punishment","summary":"Lycurgus attacks the nymph Ambrosia, who is taken into the Earth and becomes a vine that binds his neck; given voice by Rhea, she taunts him, likening herself to the remora. The Bacchants scourge and tear at him with thorns, goads and ivy, while Ares takes away the axe for fear of it being turned against his son. Poseidon shakes Arabia with his trident and the Nysian women, maddened by Megaera, kill their own children. Lycurgus still defies the gods; Hera frees him with Ares' sword and he is later worshipped by the Arabs, but Zeus makes him a blind wanderer.","ideas":["Ambrosia changes into a vine and fetters Lycurgus","Earthquake and child-killing madness strike Arabia","Hera frees Lycurgus; the Arabs later make him a god","Zeus blinds him"],"terms":[{"t":"Ambrosia","d":"A nurse of Dionysus who becomes a vine to fight Lycurgus."}]},{"i":9177,"j":9352,"loc":"Book XXI, lines 170–345","title":"Dionysus under the sea; Deriades' defiance","summary":"In Nereus' court Ino sings for Dionysus and Melicertes pours his wine, while on land the Bacchants, Satyrs and Pans grieve for him until Scelmis announces his return. The herald, shaped like a Satyr, reaches Deriades, who laughs at him and boasts that his father Hydaspes could quench Zeus' fire. Deriades refuses the wine, saying his only gods are Earth and Water and his drink is the spear, and sends back a tablet daring Dionysus to fight. Dionysus, back from the sea and told everything by Proteus, orders the Rhadamanes to build ships and marches east; Deriades splits his army across the Hydaspes and lays an ambush in a dense wood.","ideas":["Ino and Melicertes host Dionysus beneath the waves","Deriades worships only Earth and Water","Written challenge on a double tablet","Indian ambush laid in a sunless wood by the Hydaspes"],"terms":[{"t":"Deriades","d":"King of the Indians, son of the river Hydaspes, chief enemy of Dionysus."},{"t":"Rhadamanes","d":"Cretan exiles in Arabia whom Dionysus orders to build a fleet."}]},{"i":9353,"j":9499,"loc":"Book XXII, lines 1–145","title":"Wonders at the Hydaspes; the ambush revealed","summary":"When the Bacchic army reaches the Hydaspes, nature breaks into wonders: springs run with milk, rocks gush wine, honey and oil flow, and beasts dance together peaceably. A hidden Indian scout reports this and the Indians nearly surrender, but Hera bewitches them and warns them not to drink the river. A Hamadryad disguised as a Bacchant whispers the ambush to Dionysus and tells him not to cross. He quietly arms his men, who eat in armor and drink water rather than wine; Zeus's night rain checks the attack, and at dawn the Bacchoi feign retreat to draw Thureus and the Indians onto the plain.","ideas":["Milk, wine, honey and oil spring from the land","Peace among beasts at the god's coming","A Hamadryad betrays the ambush","Feigned flight draws the Indians into the open"],"terms":[{"t":"Hamadryad","d":"A tree nymph; here an Indian one who sides with Dionysus."},{"t":"Thureus","d":"Indian champion who leads the ambush."}]},{"i":9500,"j":9755,"loc":"Book XXII, lines 146–401","title":"Battle on the plain and Aeacus in the river","summary":"Dionysus routs the Indians with his thyrsus, and Oeagrus mows down their ranks with his Bistonian spear. Aeacus, guarded by Athena with clouds, kills crowds of Indians, and Earth laments her slain sons; an arrow only grazes him. Erechtheus kills a footman who faces his horse and then shoots nine men with nine arrows. Aeacus drives the Indians into the Hydaspes, refusing suppliants and reddening the water, a battle that foreshadows Achilles at the Scamander, until a Naiad begs him to spare the river and leaves for the sea.","ideas":["Oeagrus compared to a river in flood","Aeacus refuses all suppliants in the river","The fight anticipates Achilles' river battle at Troy","A Naiad abandons the bloodied river"],"terms":[{"t":"Aeacus","d":"Son of Zeus and Aegina, grandfather of Achilles, fighting for Dionysus."}]},{"i":9756,"j":9873,"loc":"Book XXIII, lines 1–116","title":"Slaughter in the Hydaspes","summary":"Aeacus and Dionysus kill the Indians trapped in the river, the god striking swimmers and men stuck in the silt with his thyrsus. A proud Indian, seeing the dead, stabs himself in imitation of Orontes rather than die by a womanish thyrsus. A drowning Indian reproaches his father Hydaspes for entombing his own people, contrasting other rivers that spare their own. Shields and helmets float off with the dead, and Dionysus kills all but Thureus, whom he leaves as a witness of the victory.","ideas":["An Indian's suicide modeled on Orontes","A drowning man's complaint against the river","Only Thureus is spared, as a witness"],"terms":[]},{"i":9874,"j":10077,"loc":"Book XXIII, lines 117–320","title":"Crossing the Hydaspes; river fights and burns","summary":"Hera stirs the Hydaspes against Dionysus, whose army crosses by every means: on leopards over the water, rafts, boats, logs, shields, swimming horses and inflated skins, with Pan walking on the surface. Hydaspes calls on his brother river and Aeolus, then floods against the Bacchants, sweeping away sandals, robes, Maron's wineskin and Silenus. Dionysus warns him with the fate of Phaethon and Eridanus, then lights a fennel-stalk from the sun and sets the river on fire, scorching reeds, fish and silt and driving off the Naiads. Ocean cries out to Tethys, threatening to flood the stars and drag the watery constellations back into the sea.","ideas":["Miraculous crossing of the river","The river's flood compared to Scamander against Achilles","Dionysus sets the Hydaspes ablaze","Ocean threatens to drown the heavens"],"terms":[{"t":"Hydaspes","d":"Indian river god, father of Deriades, who fights Dionysus with his flood."}]},{"i":10078,"j":10221,"loc":"Book XXIV, lines 1–142","title":"Hydaspes submits; the gods rescue their own","summary":"Zeus and Hera check the fire and Ocean's anger, and Hydaspes begs Dionysus for mercy, pleading his love for his son Deriades and asking him to spare the reeds that prop the vine and make flutes. He recalls bathing the earlier Dionysus, Zagreus, and calls himself brother of Pactolus; Dionysus puts out his torch and the north wind cools the river. When Deriades arms his men at the bank, Zeus and the Olympians rescue their own: Zeus as an eagle carries Aeacus, Apollo saves Aristaeus, Hermes saves Pan, and Calliope, Hephaestus, Athena and others save Oeagrus, the Cabeiri and Erechtheus. The daughters of Cydnus save the Bassarids, and the army crosses and camps, hunting and playing in the Indian woods.","ideas":["Hydaspes' plea for his river and its reeds","Mention of Zagreus, the first Dionysus","Each god rescues a favorite from the crossing","The Bacchic camp beyond the river"],"terms":[{"t":"Zagreus","d":"The earlier Dionysus, son of Zeus, whom Hydaspes says his nymphs once tended."}]},{"i":10222,"j":10427,"loc":"Book XXIV, lines 143–348","title":"Indian mourning; Leucus sings Aphrodite at the loom","summary":"Thureus tells Deriades of the ambush's failure and the slaughter, urges him to consult the Brahmans on whether Dionysus is god or mortal, and persuades him to put off battle until morning. The Indian city laments: an old man cuts his hair for four sons killed by Aeacus, and a pregnant widow swears never to drink the Hydaspes that hid her husband. In the Bacchic camp, at a feast of slaughtered cattle, Leucus of Lesbos sings of Zeus defeating Cronus and then, at Lapethus' request, of Aphrodite taking up Athena's loom. Her clumsy weaving halts marriage and births until Athena complains, Hermes mocks her, and she returns to Cyprus ashamed; the army then sleeps, dreaming of battle.","ideas":["Thureus' report and advice to consult the Brahmans","Lament of the Indian women","Aphrodite's weaving brings the world's marriages to a stop","Hermes mocks Aphrodite's work at the loom"],"terms":[{"t":"kestos (cestus)","d":"Aphrodite's girdle of desire, which she lays aside for the shuttle in Leucus' song."}]},{"i":10428,"j":10699,"loc":"Book XXV, lines 1–270","title":"Proem: Dionysus surpasses Perseus, Minos and Heracles","summary":"The poet announces that, like Homer, he will sing only the seventh and last year of the Indian war, and calls on the Muse. He compares Dionysus' deeds with those of the other sons of Zeus: Perseus beheaded only one sleeping Gorgon and killed a sea-monster, while Bacchus defeated armies on land and water and the Giants. He contrasts Danae and Andromeda with Semele's place in Olympus and Ariadne's Crown, Minos' victory won through Scylla's love with Dionysus' toil, and Heracles' labors one by one with the god's childhood taming of lions and his victories over Giants like Alpos. He closes by invoking Homer, saying his Muse should have sung this war.","ideas":["The Homeric plan: only the last year of the war","Perseus' single Gorgon against Bacchus' armies","Heracles' labors ranked below the god's deeds","Appeal to Homer for inspiration"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The rhetorical weighing of heroes against one another that shapes this proem."}]},{"i":10700,"j":11001,"loc":"Book XXV, lines 271–572","title":"Attis brings Rhea's shield; the shield described","summary":"The Indian city is afraid, and the Hydaspes now runs with wine, curing a blind old man who bathes his eyes in it. Dionysus, frustrated by Hera's delays, is visited by Attis on Rhea's lion chariot, who brings armor made by Hephaestus and Rhea's prophecy that the city will fall in the seventh year. The shield shows earth, sky, sea, sun, moon and the constellations; Thebes built by Zethus and Amphion's lyre; Ganymede pouring for Zeus under Hera's jealous gaze; and the Maeonian story of Tylus, killed by a serpent and revived by his sister Moria with the herb of Zeus after the Giant Damasen kills it. It also shows Rhea giving Cronus the stone in place of Zeus; the army marvels and sleeps.","ideas":["The river turns to wine and heals blindness","Rhea's prophecy: victory in the seventh year","Shield of Dionysus with cosmos, Thebes and Ganymede","Tylus revived by the herb that revived the serpent"],"terms":[{"t":"Attis","d":"Rhea's eunuch attendant, who brings Dionysus the divine shield."},{"t":"Damasen","d":"Giant son of Earth who kills the serpent that slew Tylus."}]},{"i":11002,"j":11145,"loc":"Book XXVI, lines 1–142","title":"Athena's false dream; the Indian muster begins","summary":"Athena appears to Deriades in a dream in the form of Orontes, chiding him for sleeping and urging him to avenge his son-in-law; she tells him Dionysus is no god, having fled Lycurgus, and that Deriades' own blood makes him invincible. At dawn Deriades sends heralds to gather allies, and the catalogue begins with Agraeus and Phlogius, the Dardae, Prasii, Salangi and the Zabii under Palthanor, who secretly favors Dionysus. Morrheus marches with his aged father Didnasus, who grieves for Orontes, along with the Uatocoetae who sleep on their ears. Tectaphus comes, once kept alive in Deriades' dungeon by his daughter Aerie, who fed him her breast milk until the king freed him.","ideas":["Athena deceives Deriades to bring on his defeat","Catalogue of Indian allies on the Homeric pattern","Morrheus seeks revenge for Orontes","Aerie nurses her starving father in prison"],"terms":[{"t":"Morrheus","d":"Indian leader, brother-in-law to the dead Orontes, eager to fight Dionysus alone."},{"t":"Aerie","d":"Daughter of Tectaphus, praised for her piety in feeding him."}]},{"i":11146,"j":11381,"loc":"Book XXVI, lines 143–378","title":"Rest of the Indian catalogue; Deriades' ancestry","summary":"The catalogue continues with the Arachotae and Dersaeans, and Habrathoos, shorn by Deriades, who fights for him by day but sends news to Dionysus by night. Then come the Cyraeans with hide boats, men from Areizanteia, where trees drip honey and the horion and catreus sing, Astraeis and Cyllarus, sons of Brongus, and Rhigbasus from the islands of the Indus, whose river-horse is compared with the Nile's. Old Aretus brings five mute sons, foretold by a sow giving birth to fish, whom Dionysus later gives voice; there are elephant-riders from Oeta, and Ethiopians who wear horse-skulls as masks. The book closes with two accounts of Deriades' parentage, by Hydaspes from Astris or Ceto, and the army sleeping in the crowded city.","ideas":["Habrathoos is a secret ally of Dionysus","Wonders of distant peoples: honey-trees, singing birds, river-horses","The mute sons of Aretus and the sow's portent","Description of elephants and the Ethiopians' horse-masks"],"terms":[{"t":"Habrathoos","d":"Indian leader humiliated by Deriades who secretly informs Dionysus."},{"t":"Astris","d":"Daughter of Helios and, in one account, mother of Deriades by Hydaspes."}]},{"i":11382,"j":11623,"loc":"Book XXVII, lines 1–240","title":"Deriades and Dionysus harangue their armies","summary":"At dawn Zeus sends a rain of blood as an omen of Indian slaughter. Deriades taunts Dionysus at length: he scorns his divine birth, threatens to enslave the Pans, the Cyclopes, Aeacus, Erechtheus and the Corybants, and boasts of his own descent from Hydaspes and Phaethon. Dionysus divides his army into four phalanxes, like the four winds, and tells the Bassarids to turn their revels into war; he threatens to dry up Hydaspes if the river helps the Indians and promises to dress the conquered Deriades in a fawnskin. The battle opens with Bacchic music, Satyrs whitened with gypsum, and Maron fighting with a vine shoot.","ideas":["Deriades mocks the miraculous births of Dionysus and Athena","The Bacchic host is drawn up in four divisions beside the Indian rivers","Dionysus offers Hydaspes the choice of becoming wine or being dried up","Revel instruments and dance become the weapons and sounds of war"],"terms":[{"t":"Hydaspes","d":"The Indian river-god, father of Deriades, who sides with the Indians."},{"t":"Bassarids","d":"The female followers of Dionysus who fight with thyrsi and ivy."}]},{"i":11624,"j":11724,"loc":"Book XXVII, lines 241–341","title":"Zeus calls the gods to help Bacchus","summary":"On Olympus, while Ganymede pours the nectar, Zeus addresses Apollo, Athena and Hephaestus. He reminds Apollo of Parnassus, which he shares with Dionysus, and of Leto's wandering labour; he urges Athena to save the Athenians who follow Bacchus, recalling Icarius, Triptolemus and Pan's future help at Marathon; and he chides Hephaestus for idleness while his sons the Cabeiri and Erechtheus are in danger. Zeus complains that Hera, Athena's rival Ares and others favour the Indians. Athena, Apollo and Hephaestus go to help Dionysus, while Hera brings Ares, Hydaspes, Terror and Panic, and Deo, jealous of the new gift of wine, to the Indian side.","ideas":["The gods divide into two camps over the Indian war","Zeus appeals to each god's ties of kinship and place with Dionysus","Deo opposes Bacchus out of rivalry over the gift of wine"],"terms":[{"t":"Cabeiri","d":"Sons of Hephaestus and the Lemnian Cabeiro who fight for Dionysus."},{"t":"Zagreus","d":"The first-born Dionysus, whose old fame the wine-bringing Bacchus outshines."}]},{"i":11725,"j":11897,"loc":"Book XXVIII, lines 1–171","title":"The general battle and its strange deaths","summary":"Faunus, Aristaeus and Aeacus lead the Bacchic charge, with riders on leopards, lions, bulls and bears and Bacchants armed only with ivy. Corymbasus, the foremost Indian after Morrheus and the king, beheads Phaleneus and Dexiochus, cuts down Clytius after his boast, and kills many more. Clytius' spear, turned by Hera, kills the elephant of Deriades. Nonnus describes marvels of death: a corpse that stays upright like a statue of Ares, and an Athenian who loses both hands yet charges on with his feet before he falls. The cavalry fight ends with a rider hurled from his horse as Bellerophon was from Pegasus.","ideas":["Corymbasus dominates the first clash","A dead warrior stands upright, still seeming to fight","A handless Athenian keeps fighting as a model of Athenian courage"],"terms":[{"t":"Corymbasus","d":"An Indian champion second only to Morrheus and Deriades."}]},{"i":11898,"j":12056,"loc":"Book XXVIII, lines 172–330","title":"The Cyclopes and Corybants enter the war","summary":"The Cyclopes fight for Dionysus with imitations of Zeus's weapons: Argilipus with an earthly thunderbolt, Steropes with a mimic lightning flash, and Brontes with a thunderous din and a manmade rain. Brontes then strikes Deriades in the chest with a crag and hurls him from his car, but Hydaspes saves his son, and Zeus laughs at the Cyclopean storm. Euryalus drives Indians into the sea, and Halimedes kills twelve men with the force of his shout. The Corybants Damneus, Ocythous, Mimas, Idaeus, Melisseus and Acmon fight in their armed dance; Morrheus' stone misses Melisseus, and the Corybants ring the chariot of Deriades with shields.","ideas":["The Cyclopes counterfeit thunder, lightning and rain on earth","Brontes nearly kills Deriades with a rock","The Corybants' armed dance recalls their guarding of the infant Zeus"],"terms":[{"t":"Cyclopes","d":"Earth-born smiths of Zeus who fight for Dionysus with fire and stone."},{"t":"Corybants (Curetes)","d":"Dancing warriors whose clashing shields once hid the infant Zeus from Cronus."}]},{"i":12057,"j":12232,"loc":"Book XXIX, lines 1–174","title":"Hymenaeus wounded and healed by Dionysus","summary":"Hera rouses Deriades, and Morrheus scatters the Satyrs. Dionysus watches the beautiful youth Hymenaeus fight, stays close to protect him, and urges him on. An Indian persuades the archer Melaneus to shoot at Dionysus; Zeus turns the arrow, which wounds Hymenaeus in the thigh. Dionysus carries the boy apart, laments him as he once lamented Ampelus, suspects Hera of arming Ares in disguise, and vows revenge. He heals the wound with ivy and wine, and Hymenaeus returns to the fight, wounds Melaneus, and follows Bacchus like a shadow.","ideas":["Dionysus' love for Hymenaeus repeats his grief for Ampelus","Zeus turns Melaneus' arrow from Dionysus onto the youth","Dionysus heals with ivy and wine instead of calling Apollo"],"terms":[{"t":"Hymenaeus","d":"A beautiful youth, son of Phlegyas, loved by Dionysus."}]},{"i":12233,"j":12439,"loc":"Book XXIX, lines 175–381","title":"Bacchic heroes fight and Ares leaves","summary":"Dionysus raises a speared Indian on his thyrsus to show Hera. Aristaeus fights as Agreus and Nomius, the Cabeiri Eurymedon and Alcon fight from a car made by Hephaestus, and Bacchants such as Eupetale and Terpsichore fight with ivy and cymbals; Maron refuses to help the old woman Trygie. Morrheus routs the Sileni, and Dionysus heals the wounded women. The Indians are driven into the Hydaspes, Dionysus challenges Deriades, and Pan rips open Melaneus. At night Rhea sends Ares a false dream saying that Hephaestus has won Aphrodite back, and Ares leaves the war in jealousy to search Lemnos, Cyprus and Olympus.","ideas":["Women of the thiasos fight and are healed by Dionysus","Pan takes vengeance on Melaneus for Hymenaeus","A dream sent by Rhea draws Ares away from the Indians"],"terms":[{"t":"Aristaeus","d":"Son of Apollo and Cyrene, keeper of bees and olives, also called Agreus and Nomius."}]},{"i":12440,"j":12626,"loc":"Book XXX, lines 1–185","title":"Eurymedon saved, Tectaphus killed","summary":"With Ares gone, Dionysus attacks fiercely. Morrheus tells Deriades he is amazed that thyrsi and leaves defeat iron, and Deriades scorns his fear. Morrheus wounds Eurymedon, whose brother Alcon shields him; Eurymedon prays to Hephaestus, who wraps Morrheus in fire until Hydaspes cools him and hides him in a mist. Morrheus kills Phlogius, a dancer who used to mime the death of Phaethon at Bacchus' feasts. Tectaphus, whom Deriades once shut in a pit, kills several Satyrs until Eurymedon splits his head; dying, he calls on his daughter, whose milk once kept him alive, and from the tower she laments and asks to die with him.","ideas":["Hephaestus and Hydaspes each rescue their own","Tectaphus, once saved by his daughter's milk, cannot escape fate twice","His daughter Aerie mourns him from the walls"],"terms":[{"t":"Tectaphus","d":"An Indian once imprisoned in a pit and kept alive by his daughter's milk."}]},{"i":12627,"j":12767,"loc":"Book XXX, lines 186–326","title":"Dionysus flees and Athena shames him back","summary":"Morrheus kills many Bacchants, among them Alcimacheia, who had once insulted Hera's statue with ivy, and the maiden Codone. Hera gives Deriades a terrifying blaze of light from his shield and helmet, and Dionysus, seeing her trick, withdraws in fear to a ridge while Deriades cuts down his army. Zeus sends Athena, who seizes Dionysus by the hair and rebukes him, comparing him unfavourably with Perseus and with the steadfast Aeacus. Encouraged, Dionysus returns, kills a hundred foes, routs several Indian tribes and wounds the hidden archer Meilanion, whom Hera rescues; the Indians flee.","ideas":["Hera's divine radiance makes Dionysus retreat","Athena reproaches Dionysus and restores his courage","The rivalry between Dionysus and Perseus as sons of Zeus"],"terms":[{"t":"Alcimacheia","d":"A Lemnian Bacchant killed by Morrheus, punished for striking Hera's image."}]},{"i":12768,"j":12871,"loc":"Book XXXI, lines 1–102","title":"Hera enlists Persephone and Megaera","summary":"Hera, angered by the Indian losses and by the sight of Perseus' monument beside the Erythraean sea, plans to lull Zeus to sleep and strike at Dionysus. She goes to Hades and stirs Persephone with complaints: Semele lives in Olympus, Zagreus was not avenged, and Bacchus may outshine the Eleusinian rites of Iacchus. Persephone lends her the Erinys Megaera. Hera shows Megaera the slaughtered Indians, calls Zeus unjust for destroying a pious people, and leaves her waiting in the Caucasus in the form of an owl until Zeus falls asleep.","ideas":["Hera's jealousy of Perseus and Dionysus","Persephone is roused by rivalry between Bacchus and Eleusinian cult","Megaera waits for Zeus to sleep before attacking"],"terms":[{"t":"Megaera","d":"One of the Erinyes, sent from Hades to drive Dionysus mad."},{"t":"Iacchus","d":"The Eleusinian Dionysus, whose honours Hera says the new Bacchus threatens."}]},{"i":12872,"j":13051,"loc":"Book XXXI, lines 103–282","title":"Iris persuades Sleep; Hera seeks the cestus","summary":"Hera sends Iris, disguised as Night, to Sleep, whom she finds haunting the doors of Pasithea at Orchomenus. Posing as his mother, Iris complains that Dionysus' torches shame the night, recalls Zeus's sleepless nights with Alcmene and Mnemosyne, and promises him Pasithea; Sleep agrees to bind Zeus for a day. Hera then visits Aphrodite on Lebanon, who guesses at a new affair of Zeus. Hera claims she fears being driven from Olympus by Semele, Ganymede and the vine, imagines drunken gods, and asks for Aphrodite's girdle to charm Zeus so she can help the Indians.","ideas":["Sleep is won by a disguised Iris and the promise of Pasithea","Hera pretends to fear that wine will corrupt Olympus","Hera asks for Aphrodite's cestus by deceit"],"terms":[{"t":"cestus","d":"Aphrodite's embroidered girdle, which makes anyone who sees its wearer fall in love."},{"t":"Pasithea","d":"One of the Graces, whom Sleep loves."}]},{"i":13052,"j":13152,"loc":"Book XXXII, lines 1–99","title":"Hera beguiles Zeus to sleep","summary":"Aphrodite gives Hera the cestus. Hera adorns herself with perfume, a crown of love-stones and herbs, and an ancient robe stained with the blood of her lost virginity, then comes to Zeus. Struck with desire, he asks whether she has come to help the Indians; she lies that she is going to recall Eros, who has abandoned marriage for love of Rhodope. Zeus answers with a list of past loves that never moved him so much, and wraps them in a golden cloud ringed by Iris' colours. Earth sends up flowers for the bed, and Sleep closes the eyes of Zeus.","ideas":["A retelling of the Homeric Deception of Zeus","Hera's adornment and the gems of her crown","Zeus's catalogue of earlier loves"],"terms":[]},{"i":13153,"j":13352,"loc":"Book XXXII, lines 100–299","title":"Madness of Dionysus and rout of his army","summary":"While Zeus sleeps, Megaera drives Dionysus mad with her snake whip and phantoms. Artemis wishes to help but is frightened off by Hera, so she only leashes her hounds to protect him. Dionysus roams the mountains like a gadfly-stung bull, butchering beasts and uprooting trees, while his followers hide. Deriades and Morrheus, aided by Ares disguised as Deriades, attack the leaderless army; Nonnus lists the fallen, and Morrheus mocks the dead Cypriot Echelaus. The whole Bacchic host flees to rocks, caves and rivers; only Aeacus holds his ground, and the Bassarids weep at the springs.","ideas":["Megaera drives Dionysus mad at Hera's command","Without Dionysus the Bacchic army collapses","A catalogue of the fallen and of the gods and heroes who flee"],"terms":[{"t":"Erinys","d":"An avenging goddess from Tartarus; here Megaera, sent to madden Dionysus."}]},{"i":13353,"j":13548,"loc":"Book XXXIII, lines 1–194","title":"Eros shoots Morrheus for Chalcomede","summary":"The Grace Pasithea sees Dionysus' madness and his routed army, and also the Bacchant Chalcomede, whose beauty she envies on Aphrodite's behalf. She tells Aphrodite, who sends Aglaia to fetch Eros. Aglaia finds him on Olympus beating Hymenaeus at a game of flicking nectar at a target, with Ganymede as judge. Eros boasts that he will avenge his mother on any god. Aphrodite complains of Helios and of Ares helping the Indians, and asks Eros to make Morrheus fall in love with Chalcomede. Eros flies to India and shoots Morrheus through Chalcomede's cheek.","ideas":["The Graces and Aphrodite intervene to help the stricken Dionysus","The game of nectar-casting between Eros and Hymenaeus","Love becomes a weapon against the Indian champion"],"terms":[{"t":"Chalcomede","d":"A beautiful Bacchant devoted to virginity, whom Morrheus comes to love."}]},{"i":13549,"j":13741,"loc":"Book XXXIII, lines 195–387","title":"Morrheus' love and Chalcomede's despair","summary":"Morrheus now follows Chalcomede with a gentle sword; on Thetis' later advice she pretends to love him and teases him with the story of Daphne. At night Morrheus, unable to sleep, laments that the cestus has defeated his shield, wishes he were a Satyr, and gazes at the constellations of Zeus's loves, longing for a disguise like Zeus's with Antiope. Chalcomede, afraid of being forced while Dionysus is away, goes to the sea and prays to drown like Melis or Britomartis. Thetis, disguised as a Bacchant, stops her, tells her to deceive Morrheus to save the army, and promises a guardian serpent that Dionysus will set among the stars.","ideas":["Morrheus abandons war in thought for love","The night sky read as a record of Zeus's loves","Thetis turns Chalcomede from suicide to deception"],"terms":[{"t":"Thetis","d":"A sea-goddess who once fled marriage with Zeus; she protects Chalcomede."}]},{"i":13742,"j":13865,"loc":"Book XXXIV, lines 1–122","title":"Morrheus confides in Hyssacus and dreams","summary":"Morrheus wavers between killing Chalcomede and carrying her off, but fears Deriades and pities his wife Cheirobie. His servant Hyssacus guesses that he loves a Bacchant and praises Chalcomede as the fairest of three hundred Graces of Dionysus. Morrheus admits his love, imagines that she is Aphrodite armed, and asks what drug could heal the wound of love. Returning to bed beside his wife, he dreams through the gate of ivory that Chalcomede comes to him, and at dawn he hails her in an extravagant speech, playing on her name as bronze and golden.","ideas":["Morrheus is torn between desire, fear and duty","He takes Chalcomede for an armed Aphrodite or a Nereid","A false dream from the gate of ivory feeds his hope"],"terms":[{"t":"gate of ivory","d":"The gate through which deceptive dreams come."}]},{"i":13866,"j":14101,"loc":"Book XXXIV, lines 123–358","title":"Bacchants captured and driven into the city","summary":"Without Dionysus the Bacchic army fights in fear and silence. Morrheus captures eleven Bassarids and gives them to Deriades; Nonnus recalls how he won Cheirobie by conquering Cilicia, where he is called Sandes Heracles. Deriades accepts the gift, warns him against love for a captive, and boasts that he will sack Lydia, Phrygia and Thebes. The captives are hanged, burned or drowned in a well, where one speaks before dying. Chalcomede, acting as a seductress, hurls a stone that smashes Cheirobie's image on Morrheus' shield, and he pursues her gently. Deriades drives the Bassarids into the city, where they lose their frenzy.","ideas":["Morrheus' past as conqueror of Cilicia","The cruel deaths of the captured Bacchants","Chalcomede's feigned love leaves Morrheus harmless"],"terms":[{"t":"Cheirobie","d":"Daughter of Deriades and wife of Morrheus."}]},{"i":14102,"j":14326,"loc":"Book XXXV, lines 1–223","title":"Fighting in the city and Chalcomede's serpent","summary":"Deriades slaughters the Bassarids inside the city while the townspeople watch from walls and roofs. An Indian falls in love with a naked girl he has killed and laments over her body, wishing for healing herbs. Protonoe, Cheirobie and Orsiboe join the fighting. Outside the wall Chalcomede tells Morrheus to strip off his armour and bathe if he wants her; he obeys and bathes in the sea, while Aphrodite mocks Ares, but the water cannot whiten his dark skin. When he tries to seize her, a serpent rises from her bosom and terrifies him.","ideas":["Beauty overcomes the warrior even in death","Indian women take up arms","Morrheus disarms himself for love and is stopped by the guardian serpent"],"terms":[{"t":"Protonoe","d":"Daughter of Deriades and widow of Orontes, who fights to avenge him."}]},{"i":14327,"j":14494,"loc":"Book XXXV, lines 224–391","title":"Zeus wakes and Hera's milk cures Dionysus","summary":"Hermes, taking the form of Dionysus, gathers the trapped Bacchants, puts the guards to sleep, and leads them out of the city; Deriades wakes to find his victory gone like a dream. Zeus wakes on the Caucasus, sees his son mad and his army routed, and threatens to bind Hera again and fetter Ares, but spares Sleep at Night's plea. He orders Hera to cure Dionysus with her milk, promising to name the Milky Way after it. Hera heals and suckles him, marvelling at his beauty. Dionysus rallies his army, promising to bind Deriades as he bound Lycurgus and to avenge Opheltes and the other dead.","ideas":["Hermes rescues the Bacchants from the city","Zeus discovers Hera's deceit and forces her to heal Dionysus","Hera's milk gives the origin of the Milky Way"],"terms":[{"t":"Milky Way","d":"The circle Zeus promises to set in the sky named for Hera's milk."}]},{"i":14495,"j":14628,"loc":"Book XXXVI, lines 1–132","title":"The gods fight among themselves","summary":"The Olympians split to help each side, and Zeus holds the scales of war. Ares strikes Athena's aegis, and she knocks him down and returns him to Hera. Artemis shoots at Hera, who catches her arrows in a cloud, breaks her bow with hail, and mocks her as a goddess of hares and childbirth, recalling Callisto. Apollo leads Artemis away and then fights Poseidon, torch and bow against trident, until Hades fears the earth will split open. Hermes stops the battle, warning that strife among the gods would make the Titans laugh and might bring Zeus's fire and flood again.","ideas":["A battle of the gods modelled on the Homeric theomachy","Hera humiliates Artemis","Hermes ends the divine civil war"],"terms":[{"t":"aegis","d":"Athena's goatskin shield bearing the Gorgon's head."}]},{"i":14629,"j":14976,"loc":"Book XXXVI, lines 133–480","title":"Dionysus' changing shapes and the truce","summary":"Deriades renews the attack and orders the burning of Bacchic camps. Dionysus' maddened beasts and serpents rout the Indians, and the giant Colletes is killed by Charopeia with a stone. Dionysus fights Deriades by turning into fire, water, a lion, a tree, a leopard and a boar; Deriades mocks these phantoms and threatens to set the Brahmans' magic against him. A vine then binds Deriades and his elephants until he begs in silence, and Dionysus frees him. The war drags into its sixth year; the Rhadamanes build ships for Dionysus, recalling Rhea's prophecy of a sea battle. Morrheus urges a naval war, and the two sides agree to a truce to bury the dead.","ideas":["Dionysus fights by changing shape","Deriades is bound by a vine and released","The war turns toward the sea and pauses for a truce"],"terms":[{"t":"Brahmans","d":"Indian sages whom Deriades says can draw down the moon by incantation."},{"t":"Rhadamanes","d":"Skilled shipbuilders who build the fleet of Dionysus."}]},{"i":14977,"j":15131,"loc":"Book XXXVII, lines 1–153","title":"Funeral of Opheltes and the games announced","summary":"During the truce the Indians bury their dead, and Dionysus has wood gathered for the pyre of Opheltes, with Faunus, son of Circe, leading the woodcutters. Mourners cut their hair; Asterius sacrifices twelve Indians, cattle and horses on the pyre. Faunus kindles fire from stones touched by lightning, and at Dionysus' call the east wind fans the flames all night. At dawn the pyre is quenched with wine, the bones are placed in a golden bowl, and the Corybants raise a tomb with an epitaph. Dionysus then sets up a chariot race, lists the prizes, and addresses the drivers on the famous Greek games.","ideas":["The funeral of Opheltes modelled on that of Patroclus","Fire is drawn from stones struck by Zeus's thunderbolts","Dionysus opens the funeral games"],"terms":[{"t":"Opheltes","d":"A Cretan warrior from Cnossus, son of Arestor, killed in the war."}]},{"i":15132,"j":15462,"loc":"Book XXXVII, lines 154–484","title":"The chariot race","summary":"Erechtheus, Actaeon, Scelmis, Faunus and Achates enter the race, and Aristaeus gives his son Actaeon long advice on driving close round the turning-post. After drawing lots they race, with Aeacus as judge. Scelmis leads with his sea-horses and taunts Erechtheus, who prays to Athena and passes him by clamping his horses' jaws. Actaeon wrecks Faunus with a trick at the turn; Faunus recovers and, mocked by Achates, breaks Achates' axle. The crowd quarrels over the result. Erechtheus wins the woman, bow and quiver; Scelmis takes the mare, Actaeon the breastplate, Faunus the shield, and Achates the gold talents.","ideas":["Modelled on the chariot race of the Iliad's funeral games","A father's lesson that skill beats speed","Tricks and taunts decide the order"],"terms":[{"t":"turning-post","d":"The stone goal round which chariots turn, where races are won or lost."}]},{"i":15463,"j":15756,"loc":"Book XXXVII, lines 485–778","title":"Boxing, wrestling, running, discus, archery, armed duel","summary":"Melisseus knocks out Eurymedon in boxing and wins the bull. In wrestling Aeacus throws Aristaeus with a trip and then pins and nearly throttles him until the heralds intervene. In the footrace Ocythous leads until he slips in the dung of the sacrificed oxen, and Erechtheus, helped by Boreas, wins the mixing-bowl. Halimedes wins the discus with a huge throw. In archery Asterius cuts the cord tying a dove to a mast, and Hymenaeus, guided by Apollo, shoots the freed bird. In the armed duel Asterius wounds Aeacus, Dionysus stops Aeacus' thrust at his throat, and both receive prizes.","ideas":["Each contest echoes Homer's funeral games for Patroclus","Comic falls, divine help and stopped bouts","Dionysus keeps the games bloodless"],"terms":[{"t":"Aeacus","d":"Son of Zeus and future father of Peleus and Telamon, winner in wrestling and the armed duel."}]},{"i":15757,"j":15924,"loc":"Book XXXVIII, lines 1–166","title":"Midday eclipse omen and Phaethon's birth","summary":"After the funeral games a truce holds until the seventh year of the war, when a midday darkness, falling fire and rain alarm the army and an eagle drops a horned serpent into the Hydaspes. The Phrygian seer Idmon, taught by Urania, reads the sign: as the serpent sank, so Deriades, horned like his father the river, will be covered by the Hydaspes. Hermes comes to reassure Dionysus, likening him to the sun that shines again after darkness, and at his brother's request begins the tale of Phaethon. Helios falls in love with Clymene, daughter of Ocean and Tethys, as she bathes; their son Phaethon is born amid starry rejoicing, and Ocean, tossing the child in play, foresees his fall but keeps silent.","ideas":["The eclipse and the eagle with the serpent as omens of victory over Deriades","Idmon's astronomical knowledge lets him stand calm while the army trembles","Hermes compares Bacchus to the sun emerging from darkness","Ocean's game with the infant Phaethon prefigures his fall into water"],"terms":[{"t":"Urania","d":"The Muse of astronomy, from whom Idmon learned the causes of eclipses and celestial signs."},{"t":"Clymene","d":"Daughter of Ocean and Tethys, bride of Helios and mother of Phaethon."}]},{"i":15925,"j":16192,"loc":"Book XXXVIII, lines 167–434","title":"Phaethon drives the sun's chariot and falls","summary":"The boy Phaethon builds a toy chariot drawn by rams and later begs Helios for his fiery car; Helios refuses, citing gods who do not wield their fathers' powers and the fate of Zagreus, but yields to tears and Clymene's pleas. He instructs his son in the planetary courses, the twelve houses of the Zodiac and the seasons, warning him to keep the accustomed path, then arms and crowns him. Gazing at the cosmos, Phaethon loses control; the horses stray, Phosphorus cries warnings, and the constellations and planets are thrown into disorder while Atlas buckles. Zeus strikes Phaethon down with a thunderbolt into the Eridanus, restores the cosmic harmony and quenches the fires with rain, then sets Phaethon among the stars as the Charioteer, raises Eridanus to heaven, and turns his sisters into weeping trees.","ideas":["Helios' lecture on the Zodiac, planets and equinoxes","The runaway chariot throws the constellations into chaos","Zeus restores order with thunderbolt and rain","Catasterism of Phaethon as the Charioteer and of the river Eridanus; the Heliades become trees"],"terms":[{"t":"Zodiac","d":"The circle of twelve houses through which Helios says the sun and planets travel, marking months and seasons."},{"t":"Eridanus","d":"The western river into which Phaethon falls, later set in the sky as a constellation."},{"t":"Heliades","d":"The daughters of Helios, Phaethon's sisters, changed into trees that drip tears beside the Eridanus."}]},{"i":16193,"j":16364,"loc":"Book XXXIX, lines 1–170","title":"Fleets gather and leaders exhort for sea battle","summary":"The ships built by the Rhadamanes arrive, led by Lycus, and Deriades, angry and shaken, summons a fleet from three hundred islands. He urges his men to burn the enemy ships with fire and denies that Dionysus is a god, scorning thyrsus, ivy and wine as no match for Zeus's thunderbolt and nectar. Dionysus rallies his champions, orders joined sea-spears, and boasts of sea allies — Poseidon, Glaucus, Melicertes, Proteus, Thetis, Ino — and of the winds, though he says he will win with thyrsi alone. The fleets engage, the Bacchic ships encircling the Indians like fish in a net, and Aeacus prays to Zeus, recalling the rain he once won for Aegina, for victory at sea and for a favorable eagle omen.","ideas":["Deriades' speech mocking Dionysus' claim to divinity","Dionysus lists his kin and allies among the sea powers","Encirclement of the Indian fleet","Aeacus' prayer recalling his rainmaking and Aegina"],"terms":[{"t":"Rhadamanes","d":"The Arabian shipbuilders whose fleet carries Dionysus' army into the sea war."},{"t":"Aeacus","d":"Son of Zeus and Aegina, ally of Dionysus, who once brought rain to a parched land by his prayers."}]},{"i":16365,"j":16601,"loc":"Book XXXIX, lines 171–407","title":"The sea battle and burning of Indian ships","summary":"Erechtheus prays to his son-in-law Boreas to blow against Deriades' ships, invoking the rape of Oreithyia. The fleets clash with heavy slaughter: corpses float, men drown in their armor, sea-beasts devour the dead, and the Cyclopes hurl cliffs at the ships. Nereids watch, Galatea fears for the absent Polyphemus, and Poseidon jealously reproaches Dionysus for not enlisting his son. Missiles fly and miss their marks; Morrheus fights from ship to ship until wounded by Dionysus' thyrsus and carried off to be healed by a Brahman. As Zeus tips the scales and the winds rage, the Cabeirus Eurymedon sets his own ship ablaze, the fire spreads through the Indian fleet, and Deriades flees inland.","ideas":["Erechtheus' appeal to Boreas as kinsman by marriage","Graphic sea slaughter and the sea as a tomb","Poseidon's complaint that Polyphemus was left out of the war","Eurymedon's fireship routs the Indian fleet"],"terms":[{"t":"Cabeiri","d":"Fire-wielding sons of Hephaestus in Dionysus' army; Eurymedon is one of them."},{"t":"Oreithyia","d":"Daughter of Erechtheus carried off by Boreas from the Ilissus, the bond Erechtheus invokes."}]},{"i":16602,"j":16817,"loc":"Book XL, lines 1–214","title":"Athena's trick and the death of Deriades","summary":"Athena, taking the shape of Morrheus, shames the fleeing Deriades by invoking his warlike wife Orsiboe and daughter Cheirobie and threatening to renounce the marriage. Deriades protests that Dionysus keeps changing into beasts, fire and water, but returns to the fight; after four missed casts he calls on Morrheus, who vanishes, and he recognizes Athena standing by Dionysus. Pursued to the banks of the Hydaspes, Deriades is grazed by the thyrsus and falls headlong into his father's stream, fulfilling the omen, and the gods return to Olympus. On the towers Orsiboe mourns; Protonoe laments both husband Orontes and father; Cheirobie rails at Morrheus for sparing his spear out of love for Chalcomede; and Orsiboe wishes to join her husband in the river.","ideas":["Athena disguised as Morrheus shames Deriades into fighting","Deriades' account of Dionysus' shape-changing","Deriades falls into the Hydaspes","Laments of Orsiboe, Protonoe and Cheirobie"],"terms":[{"t":"Cheirobie","d":"Daughter of Deriades and wife of Morrheus, who blames her husband's love for Chalcomede for her father's death."},{"t":"Protonoe","d":"Daughter of Deriades, widow of Orontes, who lamenting wishes to become a stream beside him."}]},{"i":16818,"j":17183,"loc":"Book XL, lines 215–580","title":"Victory, return, and Heracles Astrochiton at Tyre","summary":"Dionysus burns the dead on a great pyre with dirges, appoints Modaeus king of the Indians, and celebrates with feasting and dances; the army divides the spoils of gems, beasts and captives and returns home, while Asterius settles by the Phasis. Dionysus passes through Arabia and Assyria to Tyre, marveling at its purple dye, its site half island and half mainland, and the mingling of farm and sea life; he visits the house of Agenor and Europa's chamber and the springs. He hymns Heracles Astrochiton as the sun under many names, and the god appears, feasts him, and tells how the earth-born first men were told in a dream to build the first ship and sail to two wandering rocks with a burning olive, eagle, serpent and bowl. Sacrificing the eagle fixed the rocks, where they founded Tyre; Heracles adds how Eros made the spring-nymphs Abarbarea, Callirhoe and Drosera wed the earth-born, and the two gods exchange gifts.","ideas":["Funeral of the dead and appointment of Modaeus","Description of Tyre, linked to sea and land","Hymn to Heracles Astrochiton as the sun under many names","Foundation myth of Tyre: first ship, wandering rocks, sacrificed eagle"],"terms":[{"t":"Astrochiton","d":"\"Star-clad\": the title of Heracles as sun-god and city-holder of Tyre."},{"t":"Ambrosial rocks","d":"The two floating rocks bearing a fireproof olive, fixed by the eagle's blood to become Tyre's site."}]},{"i":17184,"j":17396,"loc":"Book XLI, lines 1–211","title":"Beroe's antiquity and the birth of Beroe","summary":"Dionysus plants vines on Lebanon, and the poet turns to the city of Beroe, describing its isthmus site between Lebanon and the sea. Its first men were born of earth by Nature alone, before Zeus held the lightning, and the city is claimed as older than every other and the first to receive Aphrodite fresh from the sea, where she bore Eros. The poet hails Beroe as root of life and city of laws, then gives two accounts of the nymph Beroe: daughter of Ocean and Tethys, called Amymone, or, by a younger tale, daughter of Aphrodite and Adonis. In the second, Hermes and Themis attend the birth upon a book of laws, Aeon wraps her in the robes of Justice, the beasts rejoice in peace, and Aphrodite refuses to look at the boars, foreseeing Adonis' death.","ideas":["Beroe (Berytus) as the oldest city, older than the moon","Aphrodite landed first at Beroe and bore Eros there","Two genealogies of the nymph Beroe","Her birth attended by legal symbols: Hermes, Themis, Solon's laws"],"terms":[{"t":"Beroe","d":"Both the Phoenician city (later Berytus) and the nymph for whom it is named."},{"t":"Aeon","d":"Personified Time, who wraps the newborn Beroe in the robes of Justice."}]},{"i":17397,"j":17612,"loc":"Book XLI, lines 212–427","title":"Beroe's upbringing and the tablets of Harmonia","summary":"Astraea nurses Beroe on the laws, and she grows into a huntress of such beauty that Thetis hides in shame and Zeus is restrained from wooing her only by memory of Europa, leaving her to Poseidon. Aphrodite, wishing a city named for Beroe and jealous of Athens' laws, visits Harmonia in her four-doored house shaped like the cosmos, where she is weaving the universe on a robe. Aphrodite asks which city Zeus will grant the ordinances of Justice; Harmonia leads her to the seven planetary tablets inscribed by Ophion. The Cronian tablet names Beroe as old as the cosmos, later called Berytus, lists the inventors of the arts, and foretells that under Augustus, after Cleopatra's war, Berytus will judge land and sea. Aphrodite returns and asks Eros to shoot both Poseidon and Dionysus with love for Beroe, promising him Harmonia's golden lyre.","ideas":["Astraea rears Beroe on laws and Attic honey","Harmonia's house and robe as images of the cosmos","Ophion's prophetic tablets named for the seven planets","Prophecy of Berytus as Roman seat of law under Augustus"],"terms":[{"t":"Astraea","d":"The maiden Justice, nurse of the golden race, who rears the infant Beroe."},{"t":"Ophion","d":"The ancient god whose writing on Harmonia's tablets records the fated oracles of the cosmos."},{"t":"Harmonia","d":"Here the all-mother goddess keeping the tablets of fate, not only Cadmus' bride."}]},{"i":17613,"j":17888,"loc":"Book XLII, lines 1–274","title":"Dionysus falls in love with Beroe","summary":"Eros descends like a falling star and shoots both Dionysus and Poseidon with love for Beroe on Lebanon; Poseidon withdraws, and Dionysus is left alone with the girl. He follows her secretly, kisses her footprints, drinks from the spring where she drank, and is mocked by a Naiad that no water quenches love. Jealous of Poseidon, he speaks to the empty air, then disguises himself as a young hunter but is struck with fear and can only ask whether she is Artemis; Beroe smiles, unaware of his longing. Tormented like a stung bull, he seeks advice from Pan, who, himself unhappy in love, tells him to feign modesty, praise her beauty above the goddesses, offer no gifts but his own shape, and sing tales of love such as Echo, Pitys and Adonis.","ideas":["Eros shoots the two rival suitors with a single string","Dionysus' timid, furtive courtship","The conqueror of Giants and Indians trembles before a girl","Pan's advice on wooing"],"terms":[{"t":"Pan","d":"The goat-horned god, himself in love with Echo, who counsels Dionysus on seduction."}]},{"i":17889,"j":18156,"loc":"Book XLII, lines 275–542","title":"Rival wooing and Aphrodite's contest","summary":"Asked who he is, Dionysus poses as a Lebanon farmer offering to tend her fields through the seasons, but Beroe does not understand; he handles her nets, dreams of marrying her and cannot regain the dream. At last he reveals himself as a god, urges her to obey her mother's cestus, warns of maidens punished for refusing love, and mocks Poseidon's sea gifts and the fate of his lovers; Beroe covers her ears. Poseidon then woos her, praising her above all Greek beauties and offering the sea and its gods as servants. Aphrodite, fearing both suitors and for her city, declares that they must fight for Beroe after swearing to spare the city; the gods gather to watch, and an omen of a sea-eagle snatching a dove dims Dionysus' hope.","ideas":["Dionysus' farmer speech and dream of marriage","Dionysus' revealed courtship and warnings","Poseidon's rival offer of the sea","Aphrodite sets a contest of arms for Beroe; the sea-eagle omen"],"terms":[{"t":"cestus","d":"Aphrodite's embroidered girdle of love, which Dionysus says Beroe should obey."}]},{"i":18157,"j":18301,"loc":"Book XLIII, lines 1–143","title":"Armies form for the marriage war","summary":"Ares and Hymenaeus rouse the bridal battle, with Beroe as prize; she silently dreads a sea marriage and prefers Bacchus, like Deianeira between Heracles and the river. Poseidon arms with his trident, and Dionysus rides Rhea's lion car wrapped in vines while his maenads tear apart a bull of Poseidon's herd. He sets five phalanxes under Oeneus, Helicaon, Oenopion, Staphylus and Melanthius, and harangues his followers to subdue the sea powers and turn them into Bacchic servants, sparing only Thetis. He claims to fight for Beroe's city, boasts he could bridge the sea with petrified water, and offers Poseidon only the role of singing his wedding hymn.","ideas":["Beroe's silent preference for Dionysus","Maenads dismember Poseidon's bull","Catalogue of Dionysus' five commanders","Dionysus' boast of turning the sea gods into Bacchants"],"terms":[{"t":"Mimallons","d":"A name for the Bacchic women of Dionysus' army."},{"t":"Hymenaeus","d":"The god of the wedding song, here dancing into the bridal war."}]},{"i":18302,"j":18607,"loc":"Book XLIII, lines 144–449","title":"Sea battle for Beroe and Poseidon's victory","summary":"Poseidon taunts Dionysus as the fugitive from Lycurgus and calls his Tritons and the sea powers to battle; Melicertes, Triton, Glaucus and Nereus join, Pan skips on the water chasing his echo, and Proteus changes shape in the grip of the Indians. Nereids ride fish like charioteers, the seas of the world surge together, and Bacchants, Sileni and Satyrs fight back with thyrsi, beasts and music. Psamathe prays to Zeus not to let the sea gods become slaves of Dionysus, and Zeus awards Beroe to Poseidon, checking Dionysus with thunder. Poseidon's sea wedding is celebrated with bride-gifts from Nereus and the rivers, and Eros consoles Dionysus with promises of Ariadne, Aura and Pallene; Dionysus returns to Rhea in Lydia and heads for Europe.","ideas":["Poseidon's mocking reply and the sea gods' muster","Proteus' transformations and the chaotic sea battle","Zeus gives Beroe to Poseidon and halts the war","Eros foretells Dionysus' future brides; departure for Europe"],"terms":[{"t":"Proteus","d":"The shape-changing old sea god who becomes leopard, tree, serpent, lion, boar and water when seized."},{"t":"Psamathe","d":"A Nereid whose prayer moves Zeus to end the battle in Poseidon's favor."}]},{"i":18608,"j":18927,"loc":"Book XLIV, lines 44.1–318","title":"Thebes resists Dionysus; omens and Agave's dream","summary":"Dionysus reaches Boeotia, and the rivers, springs and Nymphs dance for him, but Pentheus orders the gates shut; they open of their own accord, the palace shakes, and the statues of Athena and Ares sweat and bleed. Agave recalls a dream in which she saw Pentheus in women's dress on a tree, torn by beasts, and herself showing his head to Cadmus; Teiresias understands it but keeps silent and prescribes sacrifices on Cithaeron, where serpents coil about Cadmus and Harmonia and are turned to stone, a sign of their later change. Pentheus boasts that he will burn Bacchus, denies his divine birth, and claims a heavenly parentage of his own. Dionysus prays to the Moon under all her names, and Mene promises him vengeance, citing Lycurgus, Deriades, the Tyrrhenians and Orontes. Persephone sends the Erinyes, one of whom hides Procne's knife under a fir on Cithaeron and smears it with Gorgon's blood, and Dionysus maddens Autonoe by telling her that Actaeon lives as the bridegroom of Artemis.","ideas":["Self-opening gates and bleeding statues foretell Pentheus' fate","Agave's dream of the tree, the beasts and the severed head","Pentheus' boast that he will give Bacchus to fire","The Moon, Persephone and the Erinyes arm against Pentheus"],"terms":[{"t":"Mene / Selene","d":"The Moon goddess, whom Dionysus also addresses as Hecate, Artemis and Persephone, and who calls herself mistress of madness."},{"t":"Erinyes / Eumenides","d":"Underworld avenging spirits sent by Persephone to prepare Pentheus' death."}]},{"i":18928,"j":19287,"loc":"Book XLV, lines 45.1–358","title":"Teiresias warns Pentheus; the bull is bound","summary":"Autonoe and Agave rush to the mountain in Bacchic frenzy, Theban girls leave their looms to join them, and old Cadmus and Teiresias dance crowned with ivy. Pentheus mocks the two old men and argues that the son of Zeus would not wear fawnskins and horns. Teiresias answers with two warning tales: the Tyrrhenian pirates who bound Dionysus and were turned into dolphins as their ship sprouted vines and serpents, and the Giant Alpos, slain by a thyrsus at Pelorus. Pentheus sends soldiers, who fail to bind the vanishing god; Dionysus, disguised as a servant, brings him a bull, which Pentheus fetters in place of the god, and the imprisoned Bassarids break free to work wonders of milk, wine and tamed beasts on the mountain. The book ends with Thebes shaken and Pentheus' palace filled with a fire that water only feeds.","ideas":["Teiresias' tale of the Tyrrhenian pirates turned to dolphins","Dionysus kills the Giant Alpos with a thyrsus","Pentheus, deluded, binds a bull instead of the god","The freed Bassarids' miracles and the unquenchable palace fire"],"terms":[{"t":"Bassarids","d":"The Bacchants, the god's women followers, whom Pentheus imprisons and who free themselves."},{"t":"thyrsus","d":"The ivy-tipped fennel wand of Dionysus and his followers, used here as a weapon."}]},{"i":19288,"j":19658,"loc":"Book XLVI, lines 46.1–369","title":"Death of Pentheus and the laments of Thebes","summary":"Pentheus again disputes Dionysus' divine birth, and the god replies that the thunderbolt, not a river ordeal like the Celts' Rhine, is the witness of his parentage, then persuades him to dress as a woman to spy on the rites. Maddened by Dionysus and Selene, Pentheus puts on Agave's robe and Autonoe's veil, sees two suns and two Thebes, and is set high on a fir on Cithaeron. Agave takes him for a lion, tears up the tree, and with Autonoe and the Bacchants dismembers him despite his pleas, then proudly shows his head to Cadmus. Cadmus laments the ruin of his house; Dionysus restores Agave's mind, and she mourns, composes an epitaph, and buries her son, while Autonoe grieves that she could bury only a deer's horn for Actaeon. Dionysus soothes them with a drink of forgetfulness, sends Cadmus and Harmonia to Illyria, and revels on to Athens.","ideas":["Pentheus dressed as a woman and set on the fir tree","Agave tears up the tree and beheads her son as a 'lion'","Agave's sanity restored and her lament and epitaph","Cadmus and Harmonia exiled to Illyria, later to become serpents"],"terms":[{"t":"Cithaeron","d":"The mountain near Thebes where the Theban women hold the rites and Pentheus dies."}]},{"i":19659,"j":19924,"loc":"Book XLVII, lines 47.1–264","title":"Icarius receives wine and is murdered","summary":"Athens welcomes Dionysus with dances, and he visits the old vine-grower Icarius and his daughter Erigone, giving him wine, the gift of the vine and the art of vine-growing, and comparing him to Triptolemus with Demeter's corn. Icarius shares the wine with the country people, who praise it, grow drunk, and, believing they have been poisoned, beat him to death with farm tools; sobered, they bury him. His ghost appears to Erigone in a dream, showing his wounds. She searches the hills, finds his grave, and hangs herself from a tree, guarded by her dog, which dies at the tomb. Zeus sets them among the stars: Erigone as the Maiden with the corn-ear, Icarius as Bootes, and the dog as Sirius.","ideas":["Dionysus gives wine and viticulture to Icarius","Drunk peasants kill Icarius thinking they were drugged","Erigone's dream, search and suicide; the faithful dog","Catasterism of Erigone, Icarius and the dog"],"terms":[{"t":"Bootes","d":"The constellation into which Zeus sets Icarius' soul."},{"t":"Sirius","d":"The autumn Dog Star, set in the sky beside the heavenly Dog in memory of Erigone's dog."}]},{"i":19925,"j":20131,"loc":"Book XLVII, lines 47.265–471","title":"Ariadne abandoned on Naxos and wed","summary":"Dionysus sails to Naxos, where Theseus has just left Ariadne asleep on the shore; struck by her beauty, he silences his Bacchants and wonders aloud which goddess she might be. Ariadne wakes, finds the ship gone, and in a long lament tells of her dream of a wedding in Athens, curses the sailor and the winds, and offers to serve as Theseus' slave if only she might see him. Dionysus reveals himself and consoles her, saying that an immortal husband is better than the mortal Theseus, whom her thread had helped, and promising her a starry crown. She forgets Theseus; Eros prepares the bridal chamber, Nymphs and Ortygia sing the wedding, and Dionysus begets many children with her.","ideas":["Theseus deserts the sleeping Ariadne","Ariadne's lament against Theseus, sleep and the winds","Dionysus offers himself as an immortal husband","The wedding and the promised Crown of Ariadne"],"terms":[{"t":"Crown of Ariadne","d":"The star-garland Dionysus promises her, foreshadowed by the rose garland Eros weaves at the wedding."}]},{"i":20132,"j":20401,"loc":"Book XLVII, lines 47.472–741","title":"War with Perseus at Argos","summary":"Dionysus comes to Argos, which rejects him for fear of Hera; he drives the Argive women mad and they kill their own infants, while an Argive mocks him and claims Perseus is the greater son of Zeus. Hera, disguised as the seer Melampus, urges Perseus to fight, and the Argives arm with the weapons of their ancient heroes. Dionysus and Perseus trade boasts; Dionysus towers up to the sky, and Perseus, frightened, turns Ariadne to stone with the Gorgon's head. Dionysus would have sacked Argos had Hermes not restrained him, reminding him that Perseus is his brother, that Hera drove the fight, and that Ariadne will be honoured among the stars. Hera returns to Olympus, and Melampus persuades the Argives to worship Dionysus, ending the women's madness.","ideas":["Argive women, maddened, kill their infants","Hera as Melampus arms Perseus against Dionysus","Perseus turns Ariadne to stone with Medusa's head","Hermes makes peace between the half-brothers; Argos accepts the rites"],"terms":[{"t":"Melampus","d":"The Argive seer whose shape Hera takes to stir up war, and who afterwards leads Argos to worship Dionysus."}]},{"i":20402,"j":20640,"loc":"Book XLVIII, lines 48.1–237","title":"The Giants' attack and the wrestling for Pallene","summary":"Hera, still angry, persuades Earth to arm the Giants against Dionysus as the Titans were once armed against Zagreus; they hurl mountains, but he cuts them down with vine shoots, ivy and fire, sparing the rest for Zeus. In Thrace he wins Pallene, whose father Sithon killed every suitor who wrestled her; Cypris judges and Eros holds the garland as Dionysus wrestles the oiled girl, lingering in the hold for love of her and letting himself fall before pinning her. Sithon stops the bout, and Dionysus kills him with his thyrsus and gives the weapon to Pallene as a bride-gift. The Nereids dance at the wedding, and Dionysus consoles the mourning bride, comparing her father to Oenomaus.","ideas":["Earth sends the Giants against the later-born Dionysus","Dionysus defeats them with vines, ivy and fire","The bridal wrestling match with Pallene","Sithon, killer of suitors, slain by the thyrsus"],"terms":[{"t":"Zagreus","d":"The first-born Dionysus, killed by the Titans, whose fate Earth wishes on the later Dionysus."}]},{"i":20641,"j":20876,"loc":"Book XLVIII, lines 48.238–473","title":"Aura insults Artemis and Nemesis promises revenge","summary":"In Phrygia near Rhea's Dindymon lives Aura, daughter of the Titan Lelantus, a swift huntress who scorns love. She dreams that Eros and Cypris hunt beside her and subdue her, and on waking she rages at the laurel she slept under. While bathing with Artemis in the Sangarius, Aura mocks the goddess's womanly body as unfit for a maiden and boasts of her own manlike form. Artemis seeks out Nemesis, described with her wheel and griffin, who first guesses at other offenders before Artemis tells her of Aura. Nemesis will not turn a fellow Titan to stone but promises Aura will lose her maidenhood; she strikes Aura with her whip and stirs Dionysus to love for her.","ideas":["Aura, a man-like virgin huntress","Aura's prophetic dream of Eros the hunter","Aura mocks Artemis's body at the bath","Nemesis, with wheel and griffin, takes up Artemis's grievance"],"terms":[{"t":"Nemesis / Adrasteia","d":"The goddess of retribution who brings down the proud with her wheel, whip and griffin-drawn car."},{"t":"Lelantus","d":"A Titan, father of Aura; Nemesis spares Aura from petrification because of this kinship."}]},{"i":20877,"j":21126,"loc":"Book XLVIII, lines 48.474–723","title":"Dionysus deceives and rapes the sleeping Aura","summary":"Dionysus, consumed by hopeless love for Aura, laments like Pan pursuing Echo, and a Hamadryad tells him she can be won only bound or asleep. The ghost of Ariadne visits him in a dream, reproaching him as a faithless husband like Theseus. He recalls how he won Nicaea with wine, strikes a rock to make a spring of wine, and the thirsty Aura drinks it and falls into a drunken sleep. Guided by Eros, Dionysus hides her weapons, binds her and rapes her as she sleeps, then unbinds her and leaves. Waking to find her maidenhood lost, Aura goes mad, slaughters herdsmen, hunters and vine workers, whips and drowns Aphrodite's statue, and vows vengeance on whatever god wronged her.","ideas":["Ariadne's jealous ghost reproaches Dionysus","The wine spring that puts Aura to sleep","The rape of the sleeping, bound Aura","Aura's murderous rampage and assault on Aphrodite's shrine"],"terms":[{"t":"Nicaea","d":"An earlier victim whom Dionysus overcame with a spring of wine and sleep; the model for his trick on Aura."}]},{"i":21127,"j":21381,"loc":"Book XLVIII, lines 48.724–978","title":"Birth of Iacchus and Dionysus in Olympus","summary":"Pregnant with twins, Aura considers killing herself and her unborn children, while Artemis mocks her and Nicaea pities her as a fellow victim. She bears twins on Dindymon, and Dionysus asks Nicaea to save one son, Iacchus. Aura casts the infants to beasts, which spare them, then tosses one into the air and devours him; Artemis rescues the other. Aura drowns herself in the Sangarius and Zeus turns her into a spring, her bow and arrows becoming horn and reeds. The child is nursed by Nicaea, then by Athena at Eleusis, and Athens honours Zagreus, Bromius and Iacchus; Dionysus sets Ariadne's Crown in the sky and ascends to feast with Zeus, Apollo and Hermes, ending the poem.","ideas":["Artemis's mockery of the pregnant Aura","Aura devours one twin; Artemis saves Iacchus","Aura's metamorphosis into a spring","Iacchus honoured at Athens; Dionysus enthroned in heaven"],"terms":[{"t":"Iacchus","d":"The surviving son of Dionysus and Aura, honoured at Eleusis as a third Dionysus after Zagreus and Bromius."},{"t":"Dindymon","d":"Rhea's mountain in Phrygia, said here to be named from Aura's twin children."}]}]});